WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:21.780
Tim Ferriss: My good Dr. Walker, Matthew.
Dr. Matt Walker: Tim, it is a splendid delight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:27.180
to be with you. Thanks very much for having me.
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely. And this is for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:34.740
people listening, a very self-interested&nbsp;
conversation. You and I connected I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:42.720
not that long ago, maybe six months ago&nbsp;
or so after I finished Why We Sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:50.280
which is a spectacular book. It has as many&nbsp;
highlights I think as words in the book in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:01:00.660
my Kindle notes. And not only did it change&nbsp;
how I think about sleep, but it both gave me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.880
answers to outstanding questions, because I've&nbsp;
battled with onset insomnia my entire life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:11.280
or for as long as I can remember, certainly.&nbsp;
It gave me better questions and it gave me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:19.020
better frameworks for thinking about sleep. And&nbsp;
this conversation for folks who are wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.660
what the roadmap might look like, is intended&nbsp;
to explore areas of personal interest to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:35.040
and also several or many areas that I don't think&nbsp;
you have explored in depth in other places. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.720
we're intending to check both of those boxes.
And I thought we would start, if we can just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:49.500
skip the foreplay and jump right into the main&nbsp;
event here, discuss the connection or connections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.000
the interrelatedness between sleep and&nbsp;
Alzheimer's disease. I wanted to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.200
hop right into it. I have Alzheimer's disease&nbsp;
on both sides of my family and have seen this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:07.500
up close and personal. What are the&nbsp;
connections, if any, between those two?&nbsp;

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.840
Dr. Matt Walker: I think this is perhaps one of&nbsp;
the most exciting areas to have emerged in sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:19.860
science over the past maybe five or six years&nbsp;
or so. And just taking a step back, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:25.380
everyone knows that Alzheimer's disease, it's a&nbsp;
form of dementia typified by memory impairment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:32.400
and memory decline. And we've learned that there&nbsp;
are at least two protein pathological culprits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.400
that contribute to Alzheimer's disease. One of&nbsp;
those is called beta amyloid, which is a sticky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.040
toxic protein that builds up in the brain. And&nbsp;
the second is called tau protein. So coming back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:52.440
to your question, how are those things related&nbsp;
to sleep? Well, it's probably unfolded, gosh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:58.320
maybe in a three-part or even four-part&nbsp;
story. I'm very nervous to say four-part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.440
and you'll see why in terms of that in just a&nbsp;
second, but the three main parts were correlation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:11.880
causation, and then mechanism.
And so early on what we started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:17.220
to discover is that individuals who reported&nbsp;
sleeping six hours or less across their lifespan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:24.600
had a significantly high risk of developing high&nbsp;
amounts of this toxic beta amyloid and also tau&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:31.740
protein in the brain. Then we discovered that two&nbsp;
sleep disorders, both insomnia and sleep apnea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:36.960
which is a condition of heavy snoring, sometimes&nbsp;
you stop breathing and it's clinically diagnosed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:43.320
both of those conditions were associated with&nbsp;
a high risk of both Alzheimer's pathology. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.840
also the transition to early stages and ultimately&nbsp;
the transition to full-blown Alzheimer's disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:56.640
In fact, I think there was a recent meta-analysis&nbsp;
looking at maybe it was over 27 different studies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.340
And what they found is that people who had sleep&nbsp;
problems during their lifespan were about 3.78&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:07.980
times more likely to develop the early stages&nbsp;
of Alzheimer's disease in a premature fashion.&nbsp;

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:15.420
So those were these epidemiological studies,&nbsp;
but those are simply correlational. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:25.920
associational. They don't prove causality. So&nbsp;
correlation then went in search of causation. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.900
what we've discovered, we and other individuals,&nbsp;
some people working in animal studies, we work in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:38.820
humans, but across species what we found is that&nbsp;
if you deprive individuals of a night of sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:45.060
or even if you deprive them selectively of just&nbsp;
their deep non-REM sleep across a single night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:52.740
then the next day we can see an immediate and&nbsp;
significant increase in beta amyloid and tau&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.960
protein circulating in the bloodstream,&nbsp;
circulating in what we call the cerebral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:03.060
spinal fluid of the brain, which is this fluid&nbsp;
that bathes the brain. And also using special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:09.660
pet scanning images we've been able to see that&nbsp;
same buildup of amyloid within the brain itself.&nbsp;

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:19.320
And in fact, there was a recent study that looked&nbsp;
at the signals of metabolic detritus in the brain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:24.000
And what they found is that after one night of&nbsp;
sleep deprivation, even a full recovery night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:31.620
of sleep was not sufficient to downgrade those&nbsp;
metabolic toxins that have been building up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:41.940
brain. So in that sense, it was a demonstration&nbsp;
causally that you can remove this thing called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:48.300
sleep or even selectively excise different types&nbsp;
of sleep, and you can manipulate the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:56.700
Alzheimer's protein in the brain the next day.&nbsp;
So that was the causal evidence. Causation then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:04.740
wanted a mechanism. And perhaps here in some ways&nbsp;
you can reverse engineer it or you can flip it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:10.260
its head. If that's the bad that happens if&nbsp;
I take sleep away from you, then what is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:16.620
about sleep when we get it that deescalates&nbsp;
your Alzheimer's disease pathology risk?&nbsp;

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:23.040
And this comes onto a series of discoveries. The&nbsp;
principal person underlying this was a wonderful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:28.200
scientist at the University of Rochester called&nbsp;
Maiken Nedergaard. And she was working with mice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:35.640
And she made three stunning discoveries, in my&nbsp;
mind at least. The first is that she discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:42.240
that the brain has a cleansing system. Now,&nbsp;
it sounds strange. Many people would think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.200
"Well, it must have," because the body has&nbsp;
a cleansing system and everyone's familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:52.320
with it. It's called the lymphatic system. But we&nbsp;
didn't think that the brain had its own cleansing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.980
system. She discovered it. And it's called the&nbsp;
glymphatic system. And it's named by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:03.420
that way because of the cells that make it up.&nbsp;
They're called glial cells. They're a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:07.140
form of brain cell. We've got neurons and we've&nbsp;
got glial cells. And it's the glial cells that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:11.760
make up this network of this cleansing system.&nbsp;
So that was the first discovery that she made.&nbsp;

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:19.680
If that wasn't amazing enough, she then found two&nbsp;
more related discoveries. What she then found was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:26.220
that that cleansing mechanism in the brain is not&nbsp;
always switched on in high-flow volume across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:33.540
24-hour clock face. Instead, it was particularly&nbsp;
when those mice fell asleep and when they went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:39.420
into deep non-rapid eye movement sleep or non-REM&nbsp;
sleep, the other stage of sleep being rapid eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:44.640
movement sleep, but it was during deep non-REM&nbsp;
sleep when that pulsing cleansing system kicked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:53.940
into high gear. And so that was then her shift to&nbsp;
then say, "Well, what are those things that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:59.640
brain is cleansing during deep sleep?" And this&nbsp;
is what brings us back to Alzheimer's disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:05.400
Two of the pieces of the metabolic byproducts&nbsp;
that were being washed away by deep non-REM sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:13.140
at night were beta amyloid and tau protein, these&nbsp;
two culprits associated with Alzheimer's disease.&nbsp;

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:18.840
And scientists in Boston a couple of years ago,&nbsp;
and we've now replicated this in humans as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:23.040
identified a similar cleansing mechanism&nbsp;
in humans using special MRI scans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:30.780
So that was evidence almost from a biochemical&nbsp;
perspective. And this is probably slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:35.460
hyperbolic to say and I'm mindful of it, but&nbsp;
biochemically, perhaps it's true, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:42.120
we were starting to understand that wakefulness&nbsp;
was low-level brain damage and sleep was your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:48.840
sanitary salvation. It was almost like a good&nbsp;
night's sleep clean that was a power cleanse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:54.180
for the brain that was happening during sleep.&nbsp;
But this started to explain why you got maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:58.620
this trajectory of Alzheimer's disease. It went&nbsp;
all the way back to the associational studies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:04.260
So now we can understand why night after night,&nbsp;
if you're not getting the sleep that you need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:10.560
you're not cleansing the brain of the pathology.
And it's not vast that builds up after one night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:15.540
of sleep. And I don't want to scare anyone&nbsp;
here. And I'm sure I'll probably get some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:21.420
concerned voices. I'm not trying to make anyone&nbsp;
nervous about a bad night of sleep and the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:27.780
day you've guaranteed your Alzheimer's disease&nbsp;
fate, that that's not at all the situation. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:31.920
it did help us understand that night after&nbsp;
night, if you're not cleansing the brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:38.160
it becomes like compounding interest on a loan,&nbsp;
that it continues to escalate time and time again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:44.880
night after night. And then if that wasn't&nbsp;
depressing enough, we then went on to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:52.080
further discovery that it's a vicious cycle, that&nbsp;
Alzheimer's disease pathology, those proteins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:58.380
do not build up in the brain homogeneously. They&nbsp;
don't build up in all areas of the brain equally.&nbsp;

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:03.360
And what we discovered is that the parts&nbsp;
of the brain that start to get attacked by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:10.500
Alzheimer's disease early on are unfortunately&nbsp;
the same regions of the brain that generate deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:15.671
non-REM sleep, the same stage of sleep&nbsp;
that's associated with the cleansing.&nbsp;

00:10:15.671 --> 00:10:17.820
Tim Ferriss: That's unfortunate.
Dr. Matt Walker: Isn't it just? And so now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:22.440
we've found this vicious spiral that if&nbsp;
you don't get enough sleep each night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:27.780
you get more of that Alzheimer's build up.&nbsp;
The more that builds up, the less the brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:31.307
is capable of generating deep sleep. The&nbsp;
less deep sleep, the more that builds up.&nbsp;

00:10:31.307 --> 00:10:35.540
Tim Ferriss: What are the structures involved&nbsp;
with the production of that deep, non-REM sleep?&nbsp;

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:39.165
Dr. Matt Walker: They are a collection.&nbsp;
It's essentially a network of brain regions.&nbsp;

00:10:39.165 --> 00:10:41.940
Tim Ferriss: A constellation.
Dr. Matt Walker: Exactly. But one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:48.420
if you look at it with brain scans or electrical&nbsp;
recordings, one of the main epicenters, in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:54.420
probably the principal epicenter that generates&nbsp;
this deep sleep is a part of the frontal lobe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:59.640
particularly the middle part of your frontal lobe.&nbsp;
So if you put your finger just above your nose and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:03.900
slide it up about an inch and a half, that's the&nbsp;
middle part of what we call the prefrontal cortex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:11.460
or the medial prefrontal cortex. That is a deep&nbsp;
sleep generating center for the brain. You get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:16.920
principal dominance of your deep sleep in that&nbsp;
part of the brain. And then these big brainwaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:22.020
they splash on the brain and they actually wave&nbsp;
across the brain from the front of the brain to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:28.020
the back, from the front to the back. It's this&nbsp;
beautiful mechanism that we can see, this wave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:34.560
of these deep slow brainwaves. It's amazing.
And so that was a part of the brain that showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:41.760
these Alzheimer's attacks early on. And it's the&nbsp;
same region that is generating the deep sleep in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:46.980
adults. So that was the demonstration of a&nbsp;
mechanism that helped us perhaps go back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:53.580
explain the associations. Then perhaps&nbsp;
the fourth ingredient that I'm really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:58.380
I'm almost nervous to say it because it's&nbsp;
the most dangerous of all things, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:04.500
the suggestion of hope. And I don't mean to make&nbsp;
false promises here, but what I find interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:09.120
is that unlike many of the other features that&nbsp;
we know are associated with Alzheimer's disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:15.000
for example, changes in the physical structure&nbsp;
of the brain or even the blood flow dynamics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:19.260
of the brain, those are very difficult&nbsp;
to treat right now. And medicine doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:25.020
have any good wholesale approaches. But if&nbsp;
sleep is a missing piece in the explanatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:30.000
puzzle of aging and Alzheimer's disease,&nbsp;
then maybe we can do something about it.&nbsp;

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.900
Sleep is a modifiable factor. And we've been&nbsp;
looking at this in the laboratory. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:41.460
been approaching this not by using sleeping&nbsp;
pills. We may come onto those things. They seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:46.440
to be more blunt instruments that don't produce&nbsp;
necessarily naturalistic sleep. But we've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:52.320
developing some technology, it's called direct&nbsp;
current brain stimulation, which sounds like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.400
stuff of science fiction. It's actually science&nbsp;
fact where you apply these pads to your head and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:00.900
you insert a small amount of voltage into the&nbsp;
brain. And it's so small, by the way, that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:06.840
typically don't feel it, but it has a measurable&nbsp;
impact. And early studies demonstrated that if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:13.740
apply the stimulation during sleep, as if you are&nbsp;
singing in time with those deep sleep brainwaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:18.660
not only can you amplify the size of those&nbsp;
deep sleep brainwaves, but in doing so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:22.920
people were able to almost double the amount&nbsp;
of memory benefit that you get from sleep.&nbsp;

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:29.940
So the question then was, could we translate that&nbsp;
same affordable, potentially portable technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:36.420
into older adults and could we restore back some&nbsp;
deep sleep and salvage aspects of learning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:42.060
memory function? That's one of our goals. And&nbsp;
we've been developing this and have a startup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:48.240
company that's public now that's looking at&nbsp;
this. To me though, that is probably not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:54.060
where I'll ultimately be excited about, because&nbsp;
thinking about late stage Alzheimer's disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.060 --> 00:14:00.600
when the brain has been pathologized that much,&nbsp;
it's very difficult to salvage. I am much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:06.180
interested in shifting from a model in Alzheimer's&nbsp;
of late stage treatment to midlife prevention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:13.260
Because to me, if I look at the sleep data, that's&nbsp;
when you start to see the great depression of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:18.180
your deep sleep. It's bloody depressing, I&nbsp;
know. I'm an incredibly depressing person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:25.020
but if you look, it's in your mid to late 30s&nbsp;
that we start to see the decline in deep sleep.&nbsp;

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:33.000
So could I intervene in midlife and start&nbsp;
pushing back against the decline of deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:38.040
sleep? And in doing so, could we bend the arrow&nbsp;
of Alzheimer's disease risk down on itself?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:47.340
So that's shifting from a model of late-stage sick&nbsp;
care to a model of midlife health care. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:52.920
that's probably a much more reasonable approach.
Tim Ferriss: Matt, what causes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:59.640
decline in deep sleep, whether it's&nbsp;
absolute hours or just a percentage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:04.380
of sleep when people get into&nbsp;
their mid 30s, let's just say?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:07.440
How is that explained currently?
Dr. Matt Walker: You can already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:11.760
start to see the brain atrophy beginning.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that's it. Okay. So it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:14.880
is just overall brain atrophy.
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah. There is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:20.400
development. And there are some sex differences.&nbsp;
It turns out, unfortunately, that men seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:26.820
decline in that deep sleep earlier than women.&nbsp;
Women will hang in there. Females will hang in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:33.300
there for the deep sleep, a little bit longer than&nbsp;
men, but ultimately, by late stage, there's a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:40.440
severe decrease later in life in females. So net&nbsp;
net, they ultimately get there. But it's simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:45.000
because we start to, just like the rest of us,&nbsp;
I'm sure many people, once you get into your mid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:50.940
to late 30s, you start to think, "Wow, I'm just&nbsp;
not as robust as I used to be. I start to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:55.320
more injuries. The body starts to deteriorate."
Tim Ferriss: That's a very generous way to put it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.320 --> 00:16:00.540
robust, I'm not as robust as I used to be.
Dr. Matt Walker: Well, I'm so sensitive now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:06.540
because I think I'm about the same age as you.&nbsp;
I'm now solidly in the foothills of middle age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:11.147
So I want to be tender and sensitive&nbsp;
to myself than be politically correct.&nbsp;

00:16:11.147 --> 00:16:15.360
Tim Ferriss: You know what? I'll&nbsp;
take the tender to yourself. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:18.300
the politically correct, we can skip, but —
Dr. Matt Walker: I think it's politically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:22.307
correct. This is the language of&nbsp;
cowards and truth. So you're right.&nbsp;

00:16:22.307 --> 00:16:25.860
Tim Ferriss: All right. So we can be the&nbsp;
countervailing force. But before we get to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:31.020
gender differences — and I apologize&nbsp;
in advance. I'm going to ask a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:39.780
questions in the line of three-year-olds&nbsp;
and four-year-olds. I'm going to ask why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:49.440
and why and why, in part because I find it&nbsp;
so simultaneously exciting and depressing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:54.240
moreso exciting that we spend, let's call&nbsp;
it, and I'm not using a precise number here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:58.920
but a third of our lives asleep and&nbsp;
we, relative to so many other areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:07.740
know so little about it. It's just incredible. And&nbsp;
I find it so full of promise as a result, meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:13.320
it could represent one of the most powerful&nbsp;
levers that we can pull for different purposes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:20.640
But coming back to the age-related decline, why&nbsp;
do women hang in there longer? Is that due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:30.000
estrogen? A lower level of other hormones?&nbsp;
Other factors? Do you have any explanation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.180
for why they seem to hang in longer than men?
Dr. Matt Walker: It's a great question. Largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:41.940
unclear at this stage, that some of it&nbsp;
may again come down to brain atrophy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:47.700
that the speed of that atrophy in the brain, in&nbsp;
those sleep generating regions, is more rapid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:54.360
and more accelerated in men than it is in women.&nbsp;
Other aspects, you are absolutely right. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.360 --> 00:18:03.000
the sex hormones that we think that aspects of&nbsp;
estrogen, luteinizing hormone may be more either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:07.860
sleep protective or neuroprotective.&nbsp;
It's a little bit unclear right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:14.400
Certainly, menopause, of course, is a huge issue.&nbsp;
And once that begins, we know that the sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:21.720
issues are markedly higher in women than they are&nbsp;
in men as well. So right now, the evidence there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:29.160
is far less clear. And to come to your point,&nbsp;
you're absolutely right in terms of sleep. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:35.520
we know so much about our waking lives, but we&nbsp;
seem to know so little about our sleeping lives.&nbsp;

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:42.240
There's definitely been a remarkable amount of&nbsp;
evidence, so much so that perhaps a dimwit like me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:49.500
can try and write a book that's 130,000 words of&nbsp;
facts. But you're absolutely right. If you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:56.520
about it, 20, 30, 40 years ago, we used to ask,&nbsp;
"What are the functions of sleep?" And back then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.520 --> 00:19:03.060
the crass answer was that "We sleep to cure&nbsp;
sleepiness," which is, it is the ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:06.960
equivalent of saying, "Well, I eat to cure&nbsp;
