00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:13.920
biological and nutritional benefits of food. And&nbsp;
the same was true for sleep. In a way though, now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:18.960
20 or 30 years later, we've had to upend that&nbsp;
question based on the weight of the evidence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:25.200
Now we have to ask, "Is there any major&nbsp;
physiological system within your body or any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:31.740
major operational network of your brain that isn't&nbsp;
wonderfully enhanced by sleep when you get it or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:38.147
demonstrably impaired when you don't get enough?"&nbsp;
And, so far, the answer largely seems to be "No."&nbsp;

00:19:38.147 --> 00:19:46.620
Tim Ferriss: Are there any species, I'm going&nbsp;
to get way outside of my area of expertise here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:54.780
phylum, kingdom, are there any species that you're&nbsp;
aware of, of anything, that do not require sleep?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.780 --> 00:20:02.280
And maybe that's a poorly worded question. But&nbsp;
is there anything notable that does not require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:12.540
sleep? How far down can we go? Down to bacterium?&nbsp;
I mean, how small can we go and still see a near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:16.260
ubiquitous need for something resembling sleep?
Dr. Matt Walker: It's a lovely question. And some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:19.860
people have said that nothing in&nbsp;
biology makes sense unless you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:24.540
at it through the lens of evolution. And we&nbsp;
have certainly done that in the sleep field.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:31.980
I think it's fairly safe to say that at least in&nbsp;
every species that's been carefully studied to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:37.920
date, sleep or something that looks very&nbsp;
much like it seems to be present. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:44.760
what that means is that sleep perhaps emerged&nbsp;
with life itself on the planet and has fought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:52.200
its way through heroically every step along the&nbsp;
evolutionary path, which if that's the case, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:59.280
must mean that sleep is absolutely fundamentally&nbsp;
necessary across phylogeny. Perhaps there's one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:07.140
common theme and function of sleep. How far back&nbsp;
in our time capsule can we go through evolution?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:14.760
Certainly, we see it there in all mammalian&nbsp;
species. Then if we descend down, if we look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:22.680
birds, reptiles, and fish, we can see sleep.
We also see both types of sleep in mammals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:29.280
and in birds. We don't actually see REM&nbsp;
sleep or dream sleep in most reptiles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:39.600
An emerging controversy is perhaps there's a&nbsp;
proto version of dream sleep that comes out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:44.760
which is REM sleep, you can think of it as, in&nbsp;
reptiles. But we don't see it there. Can we go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:50.760
further back? Yes. We can see insects&nbsp;
even sleep. So fruit flies will sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:59.400
Go back even further and you can find that&nbsp;
earthworms, which are ancient in terms of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:04.680
evolutionary history, even earthworms will have&nbsp;
a period that looks very much like sleep. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:11.520
called lethargicus. And they just become immobile.&nbsp;
It literally looks like these worms go into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:17.580
state and cellular state, in fact, not just a&nbsp;
motility state, but a cellular state of sleep.&nbsp;

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:22.920
And then some people have even suggested&nbsp;
that for some forms of bacteria, as long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:27.120
as they live several days, some bacteria&nbsp;
don't live longer than 24 hours, so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:32.820
can't really ask this question, but if they live&nbsp;
for several days, they will go through cycles of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:40.320
activity and inactivity from a cellular&nbsp;
perspective, which is perhaps the predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:47.400
the precursor of these things that we call wake&nbsp;
and sleep states. You can even do a nerd out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:51.420
And I kind of put this out. I'm thinking&nbsp;
I'm desperately wrong on this one too, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.380 --> 00:23:02.100
most of us assume that sleep evolved. But why&nbsp;
should we make that assumption? Why isn't it that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:10.800
sleep was the default state when life emerged, and&nbsp;
it was wakefulness that evolved? that it was from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:16.380
the sleep state, that wakefulness emerged that we&nbsp;
came out and we started to develop wakefulness.&nbsp;

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:21.540
It's almost this assumption that sleep evolved.&nbsp;
And there was wakefulness before it, rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:25.680
the other way around. Now, I don't think we'll&nbsp;
necessarily ever be able to prove that. But it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:29.520
an interesting kind of gedanken to play with.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I love that. Did you say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.940
"G'Dunkin?" Is that what you just said?
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah, gedanken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:36.467
it's a brain experiment. It's just one those&nbsp;
[inaudible 00:23:49] philosophical experiment.&nbsp;

00:23:36.467 --> 00:23:42.300
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I see what you did there, just&nbsp;
that you pulled out some German. That's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:46.140
it was. Gedanken. I see what happened.
Dr. Matt Walker: I'm bilingual. I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:49.547
speak both British and a little&nbsp;
bit of American and that's it.&nbsp;

00:23:49.547 --> 00:23:55.920
Tim Ferriss: What I heard was "G'Dunkin," "Good&nbsp;
day" from Australia, but like G apostrophe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

