my gladiator name. But I'll put that to one side.
Dr. Matt Walker: It may actually have to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:15.240
license plate on, 'cause our mutual friend, Peter&nbsp;
Attia, we love to race cars. But based on my lap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:21.227
times, I think I need Lethargicus as my number&nbsp;
plate. Clearly, no one here is going to see it.&nbsp;

00:24:21.227 --> 00:24:25.980
Tim Ferriss: Well, all the emptor license plates&nbsp;
are taken, so you'll have to make due with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:33.720
Lethargicus. Let's revisit this direct current&nbsp;
brain stimulation for the amplification, let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:42.900
say, extension of deep sleep. Could you describe&nbsp;
in practice what this looks like over the course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:46.080
of an evening? You could also mention the company&nbsp;
name, because I know people will be interested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:54.960
And compare this to — TMS might be too much of&nbsp;
a layup in terms of comparison, but let's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.960 --> 00:25:04.800
say tDCS. So other types of transcranial or brain&nbsp;
stimulation that can be used for other purposes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:11.880
which could relate to different types of therapy&nbsp;
and treatment of depression, could relate to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:18.960
enhancement of memory in some cases. But I would&nbsp;
love to learn more of the direct current brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:24.240
stimulation and what that looks like in practice.
Dr. Matt Walker: So the complex version of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:29.700
requires you to be in the sleep laboratory, or&nbsp;
at least for us to be recording the electrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:36.600
brainwaves of your natural sleep. And then&nbsp;
essentially what we start to do is we'll develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:45.120
an algorithm that understands the cycling cadence&nbsp;
of your deep slow brainwaves. And then start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:55.620
temporally predict where the next brainwave will&nbsp;
hit in its monumental peak of explosion. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.620 --> 00:26:02.820
you time the stimulation, this is almost at the&nbsp;
millisecond level, to strike at the point of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:11.460
midnight, as it were, on that big powerful, slow&nbsp;
wave. And therefore, it's almost like acting as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:18.000
choir to a flagging lead vocalist, that these deep&nbsp;
sleep brainwaves, hundreds of thousands of cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:21.960
in your cortex, what they're doing to create these&nbsp;
deep sleep brainwaves, by the way, they all fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:25.860
together. And then they all go silent together.&nbsp;
And they all fire together and go silent.&nbsp;

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:29.400
And what we're trying to do is estimate&nbsp;
the point, the next wave, when they're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:36.840
going to fire together. And then we stimulate to&nbsp;
activate and amplify that stimulation even more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:42.480
So it requires this closed-loop feedback, where&nbsp;
you're measuring the brain and you're stimulating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:46.680
then you keep measuring, and then you stimulate
Tim Ferriss: And people are wearing caps. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:51.180
what is the format, the physical format?
Dr. Matt Walker: It's different. Sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:55.500
we're just placing electrodes on the head&nbsp;
and then the stimulation pads are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.500 --> 00:27:02.520
in between those electrodes. And you can&nbsp;
have a very stripped-down number of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:05.100
electrodes. This makes it much more —
Tim Ferriss: And now, you pointed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:10.200
for people who are not watching, you pointed&nbsp;
basically to that medial prefrontal area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:16.320
if I'm not mistaken, when you mentioned the&nbsp;
stimulation pads. So those are effectively being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:19.420
placed in over that cortical area?
Dr. Matt Walker: That's right.&nbsp;

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:21.240
Tim Ferriss: Or are they being —
Dr. Matt Walker: No, they're placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:29.340
over that cortical area. And this is why I can&nbsp;
speak about the simulation and it sounds fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:35.940
simplistic. It's actually an incredibly complex,&nbsp;
high-dimensionality problem. What I mean by that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.940 --> 00:27:41.820
is, you can ask, well firstly, where do you&nbsp;
put the pads to try to create this stimulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:46.260
whereabouts on the frontal lobe? Or do you put&nbsp;
another one somewhere at the back of the brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:52.080
so you're exiting current from the front to the&nbsp;
back? How do you do that? The next question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:59.400
how long do you stimulate for to create&nbsp;
this profile? The next component is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:04.320
frequency do you stimulate? So coming&nbsp;
back to the more complex version of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:09.000
you are measuring the brainwaves and then&nbsp;
you're stimulating in this predictive manner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:13.680
as I said, at that stroke of midnight.
What we're now starting to try and do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:20.100
is a very different approach with the stimulation&nbsp;
company. I think it's unlikely that people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:25.920
going to start strapping things on the head and&nbsp;
wishing to go to bed. And this is the reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:30.120
I'm not necessarily the biggest fan of sleep&nbsp;
trackers where you stick them on your head or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:35.460
even wristwatches, et cetera, or chest straps,&nbsp;
because we take things off to go to sleep, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:41.640
don't put things on. And so the shift that we made&nbsp;
with the technology that we've been developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.640 --> 00:28:44.280
with the company is rather than stimulating —
Tim Ferriss: What is the name of the company?&nbsp;

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:48.720
Dr. Matt Walker: The company's name is&nbsp;
StimScience. And we're just in the early stages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.720 --> 00:28:57.420
here. I am very happy to take a long time to&nbsp;
develop something that is scientifically proven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:57.420 --> 00:29:02.940
I don't want to sell snake oil out there, if it's&nbsp;
a device. My mother, for example, who's in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:09.060
elderly age is told that the stimulation device&nbsp;
is going to help her, then I don't want that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:13.800
to be scientific falsehood. So we're taking our&nbsp;
time, but the company's called StimScience. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.800 --> 00:29:18.000
people can have a look at it. What we're trying&nbsp;
to do though is something slightly different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:26.940
We're trying to stimulate before sleep for&nbsp;
about five to 10 minutes. And this is a slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.940 --> 00:29:34.020
different approach. A good analogy would be a&nbsp;
child on a swing, where to begin with you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:39.780
start pushing the child on the swing and at some&nbsp;
point they gain enough momentum that you can stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:46.260
pushing and they keep oscillating back and forth.
So now, we've taken this approach where we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:50.460
stimulating as you're cleaning your teeth, you&nbsp;
stick the headband on, you get the stimulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:55.860
and you are essentially fertilizing and&nbsp;
stimulating that territory of the brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.860 --> 00:30:03.360
to then germinate more deep sleep naturalistically&nbsp;
when you fall asleep. And what's nice about it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:11.220
when you do this stimulation for that duration&nbsp;
of time, it has an efficacious, a dose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.060 --> 00:30:17.520
impact for about two to three hours afterwards.&nbsp;
So you can stimulate and you can take it off. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:24.000
has this lingering effect, as it were. And what's&nbsp;
nice about that is that's the time when we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:29.640
most of our deep non-REM sleep. Deep non-REM&nbsp;
sleep does not keep coming cycle after cycle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:34.740
90-minute cycle after 90-minute cycle across&nbsp;
the night. You get most of your deep sleep in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.740 --> 00:30:38.040
the first half of the night, and most of&nbsp;
it in the first two hours of the night.&nbsp;

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:46.080
So we can nicely fertilize that territory of the&nbsp;
first couple of hours of deep sleep using the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:52.920
pre-sleep stimulation. And therefore, you can&nbsp;
remove the component of having to measure the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:58.920
brainwaves and do all of this fancy acrobatic&nbsp;
technology. But coming back to your question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.920 --> 00:31:00.240
I keep digressing, so —
Tim Ferriss: Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:06.300
let me pause for one second. So just, I know&nbsp;
I'm subverting my own question here. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:15.120
a few things. The first is, if we're looking at&nbsp;
the option of pushing the swing set, so to speak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:21.300
while people are brushing their teeth and so&nbsp;
on, so they're having that amplification for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:30.360
10, 15 minutes and then they go to sleep, what&nbsp;
types of clinical outcomes or measurable outcomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:36.660
would you hope to observe? I'm not asking you&nbsp;
to paint a picture of something that's pie in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:40.980
the sky, but just speculating a bit. But&nbsp;
with the information that you have, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:45.060
would you hope to observe in terms of changes?
Dr. Matt Walker: We're actually measuring these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.060 --> 00:31:51.600
outcomes. So we're doing a whole bunch of validity&nbsp;
trials within the company, essentially scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:57.180
studies. So we have outcome measures along the&nbsp;
following lines. The first is the speed with which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:00.947
you fall asleep. And it's something that we call&nbsp;
sleep onset latency. How long does it take you —&nbsp;

00:32:00.947 --> 00:32:04.920
Tim Ferriss: My nemesis.
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah. And that's a component&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:07.161
of what we call one of the flavors of insomnia.
Tim Ferriss: SOL.&nbsp;

00:32:07.161 --> 00:32:09.420
Dr. Matt Walker: Yes, SOL.
Tim Ferriss: My good friend, SOL, sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:10.620
onset latency.
Dr. Matt Walker:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:17.147
It is. And that, that's a form of what we&nbsp;
call onset insomnia or sleep onset insomnia.&nbsp;

00:32:17.147 --> 00:32:18.956
Tim Ferriss: Shit out of luck, in my case.
Dr. Matt Walker: I know, I'm so sympathetic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.956 --> 00:32:27.120
It's miserable, isn't it? And I've certainly&nbsp;
had my bouts with insomnia across the lifespan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:32.700
It is no fun at all. So one of the things that&nbsp;
we're trying to do is to see, will that pulsing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:39.000
and we're pulsing still at the slow brainwave&nbsp;
frequency, I should note. Can we increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:45.900
the drowsiness pre-sleep so that sleep&nbsp;
arrives with you with greater alacrity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:51.840
as it were, and you reduce the nice sleep onset&nbsp;
latency, as it were? That's the first thing.&nbsp;

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:57.720
The second is that we then, in our scientific,&nbsp;
studies are measuring sleep electrically and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.720 --> 00:33:01.500
we're looking to see if the amount of&nbsp;
deep sleep or the number of those deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:05.640
sleep brainwaves has increased during&nbsp;
sleep. That's another outcome measure.&nbsp;

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:12.420
A third outcome measure is the number of&nbsp;
awakenings that happen during the night.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:18.660
So one form of sleep difficulties and&nbsp;
insomnia is difficulty falling asleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:24.660
which is, as you have described publicly before,&nbsp;
something that has been a challenge for you. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:27.660
other component though is something&nbsp;
called sleep maintenance insomnia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.660 --> 00:33:32.880
which is, I can fall asleep fine, but then&nbsp;
I wake up and I can't fall back asleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:38.760
So that's something else that we're trying to&nbsp;
target just because of the clinical efficacy.&nbsp;

00:33:38.760 --> 00:33:47.100
The third component involves another actual flavor&nbsp;
of insomnia, which is not just, "I have problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:51.900
falling asleep," or, "I have problems staying&nbsp;
asleep," but you can also get a diagnosis of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:57.000
insomnia if the next day you say, "Well, I fell&nbsp;
asleep fine and I stayed asleep, but I don't feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:57.000 --> 00:34:03.360
refreshed. I don't feel restored and rejuvenated&nbsp;
by my sleep." That's a different form of insomnia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.360 --> 00:34:06.720
