00:50:41.520 --> 00:50:46.980
woke up this morning, a chemical built up in your&nbsp;
brain and that chemical is called adenosine. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:46.980 --> 00:50:52.800
more of it that builds up, the sleepier that you&nbsp;
feel. And so we think of adenosine as a signal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:52.800 --> 00:50:57.600
sleep pressure. It's not a mechanical pressure by&nbsp;
the way. It doesn't mean that at the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:57.600 --> 00:51:01.980
day, your head is nearly going to explode on the&nbsp;
basis of your adenosine. It's a chemical pressure.&nbsp;

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:11.880
Caffeine works to keep us awake by way of&nbsp;
competing with adenosine. So the longer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.880 --> 00:51:15.900
we're awake, the more adenosine is building up,&nbsp;
and that adenosine is telling your brain you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.900 --> 00:51:22.260
getting sleepier and sleepier. After about 16&nbsp;
hours of being awake, you should feel heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.260 --> 00:51:28.620
weighed down by that adenosine signal that you can&nbsp;
fall asleep easily and then you can stay asleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:37.080
Caffeine works by way of racing into the system&nbsp;
and it latches onto those adenosine receptors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:37.860 --> 00:51:43.200
But what it doesn't do is activate them.&nbsp;
Because you would think, if it's binding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:47.340
on and latching onto those welcome sites of&nbsp;
adenosine in the brain, then wouldn't that make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:47.340 --> 00:51:52.260
you more sleepy? Well, the reason it doesn't&nbsp;
is because it has the opposite effect. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:52.260 --> 00:51:58.740
not quite the opposite effect. It races in and&nbsp;
it just latches itself onto those receptors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:58.740 --> 00:52:03.600
inactivates those receptors. So it doesn't&nbsp;
inhibit the receptors, it just blocks them.&nbsp;

00:52:04.800 --> 00:52:10.140
It's almost as though caffeine is like the mute&nbsp;
button on your remote TV controller. It just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:10.140 --> 00:52:17.280
comes in and it mutes the signal of adenosine,&nbsp;
of sleepiness. So it's what we call a competitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:17.280 --> 00:52:23.820
receptor blocker, and it has very sharp elbows. It&nbsp;
will come in, it will nudge adenosine out the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:23.820 --> 00:52:29.940
latch on and hijack those receptors, and block&nbsp;
the signal of sleepiness. And that's why all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:29.940 --> 00:52:35.160
a sudden you think, gosh, I was feeling pretty&nbsp;
sleepy. I've been awake for 14 hours. I have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:35.160 --> 00:52:40.800
espresso. I don't feel sleepy anymore. It's&nbsp;
not as though you've removed the adenosine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:45.420
The adenosine is still present. Sleepiness&nbsp;
is still present, and it will continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:45.420 --> 00:52:48.900
build up the longer that you are awake. It's&nbsp;
simply that your brain is no longer getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:48.900 --> 00:52:54.720
the message of adenosine because caffeine is&nbsp;
blocking the signal, if that makes some sense.&nbsp;

00:52:54.720 --> 00:53:01.620
So that's the reason that caffeine will then&nbsp;
start to disrupt your sleep, and it will disrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:01.620 --> 00:53:06.780
your sleep in probably several different ways. The&nbsp;
first is that it will, because it's a stimulant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:06.780 --> 00:53:12.840
prolong the time it takes you to fall asleep.&nbsp;
And you mentioned that too. The other aspect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:18.000
caffeine, though, is that it's what we call&nbsp;
anxiogenic, that it increases your anxiety.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:19.800 --> 00:53:24.540
Anxiety, including what we think of as&nbsp;
physiological anxiety, biological anxiety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.540 --> 00:53:28.740
which is essentially having your fight or&nbsp;
flight branch of the nervous system switched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:28.740 --> 00:53:35.640
on into higher gear, and aspects of your stress&nbsp;
chemistry and things like cortisol, those things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:35.640 --> 00:53:43.140
will be ramped up by way of caffeine. And that is&nbsp;
the exact opposite of what you need to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:43.140 --> 00:53:47.460
fall asleep. You need to disengage the fight or&nbsp;
flight branch of the nervous system and shift over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:47.460 --> 00:53:51.360
to the more restful branch of the nervous system&nbsp;
that we call the parasympathetic nervous system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:51.900 --> 00:53:58.500
And you can't do that because of the caffeine.
So what happens is that psychologically the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:58.500 --> 00:54:02.820
caffeine is preventing you from falling asleep.&nbsp;
Then you start to get anxious because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:02.820 --> 00:54:09.660
anxiogenic, it increases anxiety. At that point,&nbsp;
you start to ruminate. This Rolodex of anxiety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:09.660 --> 00:54:16.860
begins to whirl and you start to then ruminate.&nbsp;
And when you ruminate, you catastrophize because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:16.860 --> 00:54:22.200
everything seems so much worse in the darkness&nbsp;
of night than it does in the light of day. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:22.200 --> 00:54:27.000
at that point of catastrophizing and ruminating,&nbsp;
you're dead in the water for the next two hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:28.783
as it were. So that's one of the problems.
Tim Ferriss: Story of my life.&nbsp;

00:54:28.783 --> 00:54:34.500
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah, I'm so sorry. This can&nbsp;
sound painfully familiar to many people out there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:35.160 --> 00:54:39.420
So that's one of the issues with caffeine.
The other is its duration of action. You mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:39.420 --> 00:54:46.560
its pharmacokinetics. It has a half-life of what&nbsp;
we call five to six hours, which is just a fancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.560 --> 00:54:50.700
way of saying that after about five to six hours,&nbsp;
half of the caffeine's still in your system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:50.700 --> 00:54:56.100
which means that caffeine has a quarter life of,&nbsp;
for the average adult at least, 10 to 12 hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.420 --> 00:55:02.700
And it's probably, again, not really a very&nbsp;
good analogy, but if you have a cup of coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:02.700 --> 00:55:08.820
let's say at 1:00 p.m. or 2:00 p.m. in the&nbsp;
afternoon, is it similar to then saying, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:08.820 --> 00:55:13.020
that's the equivalent of tucking myself into&nbsp;
bed at midnight before I switch the light out. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:13.020 --> 00:55:17.040
swig a quarter of a cup of coffee and I hope for a&nbsp;
good night of sleep." That's probably not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.040 --> 00:55:23.220
happen because a quarter of the caffeine is still&nbsp;
in the brain swilling around at midnight. So its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.220 --> 00:55:27.480
duration of action is something that people may&nbsp;
want to be mindful of and that will impact sleep.&nbsp;

00:55:28.500 --> 00:55:36.480
The other component is that caffeine will&nbsp;
destabilize your sleep, so it makes your sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:36.480 --> 00:55:42.840
more fragile. As a consequence, if you are prone&nbsp;
to waking up, and we all will wake up across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:42.840 --> 00:55:49.260
night, even healthy, good sleepers will wake up.&nbsp;
Because caffeine will destabilize and make your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:49.260 --> 00:55:54.540
sleep more fragile, it's more likely that you'll&nbsp;
wake up. And when you do wake up, your sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:54.540 --> 00:56:00.300
is less robust and it's harder for you to fall&nbsp;
back asleep. So now sleep maintenance, insomnia.&nbsp;

00:56:00.900 --> 00:56:07.560
And then the final part of caffeine comes back&nbsp;
to deep sleep. We've done these studies where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:07.560 --> 00:56:13.680
we can dose people at different times of the&nbsp;
day and into the evening. If you give people a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:13.680 --> 00:56:19.740
standardized dose of caffeine, maybe 150, 180,&nbsp;
or 200 milligrams, which would be, I suppose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:19.740 --> 00:56:26.520
the equivalent of probably a very strongly&nbsp;
dripped, brewed cup of coffee or probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:27.180 --> 00:56:33.900
one and a half cups of coffee, what we can see is&nbsp;
a decrease in the amount of deep non-REM sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:33.900 --> 00:56:40.020
particularly in the first two hours of the&nbsp;
night. It can decimate that deep sleep.&nbsp;

00:56:41.220 --> 00:56:44.580
In fact, there was a reduction, if you look&nbsp;
at that, and we've done some of these studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:44.580 --> 00:56:49.140
by a single cup of coffee in the evening, it will&nbsp;
drop the amount of deep sleep by about 30 percent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:51.480 --> 00:56:56.820
which to put that in context, I would&nbsp;
probably have to age you by about 12 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:56.820 --> 00:57:00.900
14 years to get that type of reduction in&nbsp;
your deep sleep. Or you could just do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:00.900 --> 00:57:05.220
every night with an espresso if you wanted to.
I do think that that's relevant, by the way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:05.220 --> 00:57:11.940
Some people will say, "Look, I can have a cup&nbsp;
of coffee with dinner or even two and I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:11.940 --> 00:57:19.440
fall asleep fine and I stay asleep. So no harm, no&nbsp;
foul." The problem there is that it discounts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:19.440 --> 00:57:25.140
idea that you have no sense of how much deep sleep&nbsp;
that you get at night. Yes, you probably remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:25.140 --> 00:57:30.360
did you struggle to fall asleep or did you wake&nbsp;
up? But none of us has a recollection of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:30.360 --> 00:57:38.460
quality of our deep slow brain wave activity, but&nbsp;
yet you may still be suffering from that excising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:38.460 --> 00:57:43.800
of a significant amount of your deep sleep.
So the next morning, you don't feel refreshed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:43.800 --> 00:57:48.240
or restored by your sleep, but you don't remember&nbsp;
struggling to fall asleep or having a hard time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:48.240 --> 00:57:52.920
staying asleep. And so you discount the idea&nbsp;
that it was the coffee the next night. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:52.920 --> 00:58:00.180
now you start reaching for three cups of coffee&nbsp;
the next morning, and then so on and so forth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:00.180 --> 00:58:04.200
The vicious cycle begins. The harder it is the&nbsp;
next night to fall asleep. The less deep sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:04.200 --> 00:58:08.520
the more coffee you get. And then people&nbsp;
start falling into the trap of alcohol or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.520 --> 00:58:11.520
sleeping aids to help them fall asleep.
Tim Ferriss: So let me hop in. Let me hop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:11.520 --> 00:58:18.840
in. I'm going to stage an intervention. All&nbsp;
right. So the stimulant depressant cycle is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:18.840 --> 00:58:22.200
a whole mess. I've been an active&nbsp;
participant on that field before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:23.280 --> 00:58:27.840
But if I could just return to some&nbsp;
of the questions that kicked us off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:28.800 --> 00:58:40.320
So why allow or endorse the idea of a cup of&nbsp;
coffee in the morning, number one, if it is after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:40.320 --> 00:58:48.540
this litany of sins? And then how could coffee&nbsp;
be associated with any of the health benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:48.540 --> 00:58:52.980
of sleep? And if so, how is that the case?
Dr. Matt Walker: You're absolutely right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:52.980 --> 00:58:57.540
I think at the time when I was writing&nbsp;
the book a few years ago, the evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.540 --> 00:59:03.780
was starting to emerge there that drinking&nbsp;
coffee had health benefits. And there's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:03.780 --> 00:59:10.620
some great meta-analyses quite recently, and it is&nbsp;
striking. And you just can't really deny it on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:10.620 --> 00:59:17.580
strength of the evidence that drinking coffee is&nbsp;
associated with numerous health benefits and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:17.580 --> 00:59:23.700
reduction in risk for numerous health conditions.
And what's striking, as you mentioned elegantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:23.700 --> 00:59:28.920
is that many of the same health-related&nbsp;
conditions that drinking coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:29.700 --> 00:59:37.860
is associated with reducing are the very&nbsp;
same diseases that sleep will also reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:37.860 --> 00:59:43.740
in terms of your risk. So how on earth does&nbsp;
this work? They seem completely paradoxical.&nbsp;

00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:54.840
The answer is antioxidants. Because it turns&nbsp;
out that the coffee bean itself contains much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:54.840 --> 01:00:00.600
more than just caffeine. It contains&nbsp;
a very healthy dose of antioxidants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:01.380 --> 01:00:08.460
a family called the polyphenols. Perhaps the&nbsp;
principal one is — well, there's a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:08.460 --> 01:00:14.340
different polyphenols that it contains, but&nbsp;
chlorogenic acids are probably the principal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:16.920 --> 01:00:23.220
ester that carries some of these health benefits.
So what we realized is that the coffee bean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:23.220 --> 01:00:28.860
because most people in developed nations are&nbsp;
still deficient in the whole food dietary intake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:28.860 --> 01:00:36.240
the humble coffee bean has been asked to carry&nbsp;
the Herculean weight of all of our antioxidant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:43.500
needs. And that's why drinking coffee has such a&nbsp;
strong statistical health signal in the data when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:43.500 --> 01:00:49.320
you do epidemiological studies. So it's not the&nbsp;
caffeine that's related to the health benefits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:49.320 --> 01:00:53.940
it's the antioxidants. In case in point,&nbsp;
if you look at de-caffeinated coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:53.940 --> 01:00:57.540
you get many of the same health —
Tim Ferriss: I was just going to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:57.540 --> 01:01:03.480
I hate to spoil the party with a question. If&nbsp;
I could jump in for a second, just a quick side&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:03.480 --> 01:01:10.620
note. So the antioxidant and nutritional&nbsp;
value of coffee bean in, let's just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.640 --> 01:01:17.580
less industrialized or lower income&nbsp;
strata of various countries is true&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:17.580 --> 01:01:23.220
also for cocoa in the Peruvian Andes&nbsp;
and elsewhere. It's actually a source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:23.880 --> 01:01:29.520
very important nutrition for a lot of&nbsp;
these communities and indigenous groups.&nbsp;

01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:34.920
So I just wanted to say that as an aside. Also,&nbsp;
chlorogenic acid, I think, is contained in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:41.460
quite a few other compounds and beverages, if&nbsp;
I'm not mistaken. So I want to say that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:42.060 --> 01:01:48.240
present in yerba mate, which they drink all the&nbsp;
time in Argentina. I may be getting that wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:48.240 --> 01:01:52.860
so somebody can fact-check me. But is&nbsp;
chlorogenic acid found in camellia sinensis&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:52.860 --> 01:01:58.980
tea plants or other types of beverages?&nbsp;
Or is it particularly prevalent in coffee?&nbsp;

01:01:58.980 --> 01:02:03.300
Dr. Matt Walker: No, you can find it. It's&nbsp;
certainly nowhere near exclusive to the coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:03.300 --> 01:02:11.520
bean itself. By the way, it doesn't contain any&nbsp;
chloride. Please don't be worried about drinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:11.520 --> 01:02:16.440
in bleach or something like that. It's got nothing&nbsp;
to do with that. But yep, the chlorogenic acids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:16.440 --> 01:02:20.280
that's certainly one group. It's not to say&nbsp;
it's the only group though. There are others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:21.240 --> 01:02:26.940
Acrylamide is another one that we've been&nbsp;
very interested in terms of the coffee bean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:26.940 --> 01:02:30.300
which is another antioxidants.&nbsp;
So it's a cluster of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:30.300 --> 01:02:37.140
antioxidants that provide these benefits.
Tim Ferriss: Any brewing methods, roasts, grinds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:37.140 --> 01:02:43.620
any combination of those variables that if one&nbsp;
wanted to maximize for the good stuff and minimize&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:45.900 --> 01:02:53.700
the potential damage to sleep and sleep&nbsp;
architecture? Any thoughts on what that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:53.700 --> 01:02:57.480
Goldilocks combination might look like?
Dr. Matt Walker: It is interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:57.480 --> 01:03:02.460
and by the way, I think the Goldilocks combination&nbsp;
comes onto the idea that when it comes to coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:02.460 --> 01:03:09.120
it's the dose and the timing that make the&nbsp;
poison here. Obviously, if you look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:09.120 --> 01:03:12.900
health benefits too, once you get past about&nbsp;
two and a half, three cups of coffee a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.900 --> 01:03:17.400
the health benefits start to go down in the&nbsp;
opposite direction. So it's not a linear&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:17.400 --> 01:03:21.300
relationship. Don't start drinking like seven&nbsp;
cups of coffee, and be mindful of the timing.&nbsp;

01:03:21.300 --> 01:03:26.580
But to come to your question, I suppose, if we're&nbsp;
talking about caffeine concentration and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:26.580 --> 01:03:32.640
maybe antioxidant concentration, actually here I&nbsp;
am going to do a Peter Attia. I'm a two by three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:32.640 --> 01:03:37.260
because you could think about the rows of this&nbsp;
table being the caffeine and the antioxidants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:37.260 --> 01:03:43.320
And then the columns, three columns would&nbsp;
be the roast maybe of the coffee bean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:43.320 --> 01:03:48.900
the grind of the coffee bean, the granularity,&nbsp;
the coarseness, and then maybe the brewing method.&nbsp;

01:03:50.280 --> 01:03:55.440
It's not quite as simple as this, but certainly&nbsp;
what we found is that for the roast of the coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:55.440 --> 01:04:00.480
bean, and this comes onto the color of the coffee&nbsp;
bean, a coffee bean is a coffee bean in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:02.640 --> 01:04:09.000
when it comes out, what changes its color is&nbsp;
how you roast it. And what we found is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:10.500 --> 01:04:17.940
gram for gram, light roast actually has about the&nbsp;
same caffeine content as dark roast. But the issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:17.940 --> 01:04:23.340
is that the dark roast, the longer that you roast&nbsp;
it, the more degraded the coffee bean becomes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:23.340 --> 01:04:30.480
hence the lighter its density. So net-net on&nbsp;
average, a lighter roast will contain more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:30.480 --> 01:04:34.680
caffeine than a darker roast. So it's a little&nbsp;
bit complex. [inaudible 01:05:39] that's clear.&nbsp;

01:04:34.680 --> 01:04:43.320
In terms of the grind, I think it's fairly&nbsp;
clear that fine-grain coffee produces higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:43.320 --> 01:04:48.360
degree of caffeine concentration than a&nbsp;
coarse grain. Now of course, we're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:48.360 --> 01:04:51.900
talking about brewing methods yet, but that's&nbsp;
simply probably on the basis of surface area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:51.900 --> 01:04:55.680
that the finer the grain, the greater the surface&nbsp;
area, the greater the release of the caffeine.&nbsp;

01:04:56.640 --> 01:05:01.680
Brewing method, it's really interesting&nbsp;
if you look at some of the data. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:01.680 --> 01:05:06.180
longer the brewing method, the greater the&nbsp;
caffeine concentration relative to shorter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:06.780 --> 01:05:15.300
Also, cold brews tend to produce a stronger&nbsp;
caffeine content than hot brews. I think part&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:15.300 --> 01:05:21.180
of that simply is down to the duration of the&nbsp;
brew itself. Cold brews typically take longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:29.820
and therefore you get a stronger pound for the&nbsp;
punch in terms of caffeine. So that's caffeine.&nbsp;

01:05:29.820 --> 01:05:39.960
Antioxidants. In terms of the chlorogenic acids,&nbsp;
you're probably going to favor lighter rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:39.960 --> 01:05:44.700
darker roasts. Lighter roasts typically&nbsp;
have higher amounts of chlorogenic acid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:45.660 --> 01:05:51.900
than darker roasts, although there is some&nbsp;
evidence that darker roasts have higher amounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:51.900 --> 01:05:58.500
of some of the other antioxidants like acrylamide,&nbsp;
for example. So I don't think you need to worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:58.500 --> 01:06:05.340
too much in terms of the antioxidants. And also,&nbsp;
by the way, thankfully, the decaffeinating process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:06.180 --> 01:06:10.140
still preserves the antioxidants, and&nbsp;
that's why it's still related to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:10.140 --> 01:06:14.880
health benefits. You don't lose out on the&nbsp;
antioxidants when you switch to decaffeinated.&nbsp;

01:06:16.500 --> 01:06:22.500
Finer grinds typically produce more antioxidants&nbsp;
than coarser grind in terms of that. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:22.500 --> 01:06:29.640
brewing method, it's probably that cold brew seems&nbsp;
to produce stronger antioxidant concentrations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:29.640 --> 01:06:39.060
Then probably the next down would be espresso&nbsp;
preparation. Then instant coffee seems to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:39.060 --> 01:06:46.680
finally higher concentrations of antioxidants&nbsp;
than drip or infusion bag versions. So I'm sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:46.680 --> 01:06:49.847
that I'll stand corrected by the internet,&nbsp;
but that's my reading of the literature.&nbsp;

01:06:49.847 --> 01:07:01.200
Tim Ferriss: Perfect. So let's go from one&nbsp;
socially acceptable psychoactive to one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:01.200 --> 01:07:06.180
is certainly becoming more and more socially&nbsp;
acceptable that I'd like to ask about next.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:06.180 --> 01:07:15.000
And that is cannabis, broadly speaking, but&nbsp;
specifically in this case CBD, THC, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:15.000 --> 01:07:22.260
potentially CBN, if you have any thoughts on it,&nbsp;
because it is commonly sold as for sleep, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.260 --> 01:07:31.200
don't know how much that is supported in any way.
So let's talk about the CBD, THC. And this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:31.200 --> 01:07:39.840
personal interest to me because I find that&nbsp;
THC specifically helps me tremendously with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:40.740 --> 01:07:46.560
turning down the volume on the rumination&nbsp;
that tends to be associated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:47.400 --> 01:07:54.060
really horrible sleep onset insomnia. It&nbsp;
tones down the chatter tremendously for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:54.060 --> 01:08:01.440
and I find that at least subjectively to be one&nbsp;
of the primary reasons it helps me to sleep.&nbsp;

01:08:01.440 --> 01:08:10.860
However, I've often wondered if I'm taking, say,&nbsp;
what I would consider smaller doses in the forms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:10.860 --> 01:08:16.440
of edibles, which are then converted through&nbsp;
first-pass metabolism into other things, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:17.580 --> 01:08:25.320
at 2.5 milligrams or five milligrams or 7.5&nbsp;
milligrams, am I inhibiting my REM sleep?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:25.320 --> 01:08:29.880
Coming back to what you said earlier, sometimes&nbsp;
you don't know what you don't know because you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:29.880 --> 01:08:34.860
not immediately aware of how much REM sleep you're&nbsp;
getting unless you're tracking it using some type&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:34.860 --> 01:08:44.520
of biometric device. Am I screwing myself in the&nbsp;
long term by using this aid in the short term? So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:44.520 --> 01:08:50.940
that's the context, but please, in any way that&nbsp;
makes sense, tell us what you know about CBD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:50.940 --> 01:08:58.860
THC and anything else related to this fine plant.
Dr. Matt Walker: So there's probably more research&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:58.860 --> 01:09:03.180
that we have right now on THC, which is&nbsp;
the psychoactive component of cannabis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:03.780 --> 01:09:10.260
but there is some fairly interesting data on CBD,&nbsp;
too, and a little significantly less data on CBN.&nbsp;

01:09:13.320 --> 01:09:18.540
We have a set of cannabinoid receptors in the&nbsp;
brain, both what's called the CB1 receptor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:18.540 --> 01:09:23.040
the CB2 receptor. The CB1 receptor is the primary&nbsp;
one that's expressed in the brain. That's where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:23.040 --> 01:09:33.060
we think THC is having its sleep effect. And&nbsp;
by the way, if you go back, some of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:33.060 --> 01:09:39.900
writings of the first emergence of this story&nbsp;
of THC for sleep, at least from what I found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:39.900 --> 01:09:46.860
came from the writings of this brilliant British&nbsp;
physician, Dr. Clendinning. He published his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:46.860 --> 01:09:53.580
first monographs in 1843, I think it was. And&nbsp;
if you read it, I think you would love it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:53.580 --> 01:09:55.791
Tim. I should send you some of these articles.
Tim Ferriss: Before it was cool.&nbsp;

01:09:55.791 --> 01:09:58.410
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah, before it was cool.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:58.410 --> 01:10:00.300
on it before it was cool.
Dr. Matt Walker: It was hemp&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:00.300 --> 01:10:05.700
resin this that he was talking about. But just&nbsp;
the writing itself you would love. It's this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:05.700 --> 01:10:11.400
wonderful — oh, gosh, I wish I could write that&nbsp;
way. But his writing is lovely and delicious, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:11.400 --> 01:10:16.800
I could read it all weekend. But he was the first&nbsp;
to describe some soporific and sedating benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:16.800 --> 01:10:21.960
of hemp resin. And then another doctor the same&nbsp;
year, O'Shaughnessy published some evidence too.&nbsp;

01:10:21.960 --> 01:10:30.240
What we probably know are four things related&nbsp;
to THC with a possible caveat. The first is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.240 --> 01:10:35.040
that you get a fairly reliable, very nice&nbsp;
reduction in the time it takes you to fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:35.040 --> 01:10:42.240
asleep. And this is just what you are describing&nbsp;
too. It seems to have sedative benefit. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:42.240 --> 01:10:47.580
we're starting to unpack and understand&nbsp;
exactly why THC has that sedating effect.&nbsp;

01:10:49.200 --> 01:10:55.320
The next issue, however, isn't quite such&nbsp;
rosy news, that you seem to develop tolerance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:55.320 --> 01:10:59.760
which is that the same dose,&nbsp;
if you're using it chronically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:59.760 --> 01:11:05.100
consistently every single night,&nbsp;
can in some individuals lead to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:06.420 --> 01:11:12.480
a tolerance. That means that you have to increase&nbsp;
the dose, and therefore, you develop a dependency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:12.480 --> 01:11:17.280
And I think the way if people want to test&nbsp;
dependency would be to say, look, if I have to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:17.280 --> 01:11:23.640
let's say, travel internationally, and I obviously&nbsp;
don't want to transit illicit drugs into other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:23.640 --> 01:11:28.860
countries if they're not legal there, then would&nbsp;
I start to just get very anxious? I would think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:28.860 --> 01:11:35.100
well, my goodness, I've lost my crutch of THC, and&nbsp;
at that point, it probably demonstrates that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:35.100 --> 01:11:41.820
is a dependency that you have on it, which I'd&nbsp;
prefer or wish for people not to have necessarily.&nbsp;

01:11:42.480 --> 01:11:52.020
The third feature of THC is that it has quite a&nbsp;
significant withdrawal rebound effect. So when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.020 --> 01:11:57.780
people stop using THC, they typically don't&nbsp;
just go back to the bad sleep that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:57.780 --> 01:12:03.180
having before. It's often even worse.
Tim Ferriss: Do we have any idea of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:04.140 --> 01:12:12.960
dosage range that we're talking about, right?&nbsp;
Because you could develop DT and tremors and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:12.960 --> 01:12:17.460
on from alcohol withdrawal, but that's not going&nbsp;
to happen if you're having one glass of wine per&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:17.460 --> 01:12:27.180
night. So in this particular case with THC, how&nbsp;
much does one need to be taking, or do we know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:27.180 --> 01:12:34.140
on a consistent, let's just say, nightly&nbsp;
basis to experience that type of rebound&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:34.140 --> 01:12:39.840
that would be so harmful to sleep? Do you know?
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah, I wish I had an answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:39.840 --> 01:12:44.880
and we currently just simply don't know.&nbsp;
There are not enough systematics. Right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:44.880 --> 01:12:47.100
it's more simply survey studies where people —
Tim Ferriss: But it's observable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:47.100 --> 01:12:49.620
like it can happen.
Dr. Matt Walker: I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.620 --> 01:12:56.220
it's so observable. It's so reliable, this rebound&nbsp;
insomnia withdrawal, that it forms part of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:56.220 --> 01:13:01.260
DSM, The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual&nbsp;
of Mental Disorders, the psychiatry Bible. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:01.260 --> 01:13:08.460
forms part of the criteria symptoms for cannabis&nbsp;
withdrawal, this insomnia syndrome. That's how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:08.460 --> 01:13:10.003
consistent and reliable it is.
Tim Ferriss: Wow.&nbsp;

01:13:10.003 --> 01:13:15.480
Dr. Matt Walker: It's part of the diagnostic&nbsp;
criteria. And in fact, often it's the insomnia&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:15.480 --> 01:13:20.820
that happens when you stop using it that&nbsp;
is one of the prime reasons for remittance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:20.820 --> 01:13:27.107
that people will then go back to using it because&nbsp;
they just dislike the insomnia so much. And so —&nbsp;

01:13:27.107 --> 01:13:29.599
Tim Ferriss: Go back to the wacky&nbsp;
tobacco. They can't stay away.&nbsp;

01:13:29.599 --> 01:13:36.180
Dr. Matt Walker: And so you get that —
And then the other aspect is that it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.180 --> 01:13:42.780
to quite significantly disrupt your REM sleep,&nbsp;
and it does it in three ways. The first is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.780 --> 01:13:48.360
it will decrease the overall amount of REM sleep&nbsp;
that you are getting. The second is that it delays&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:48.360 --> 01:13:58.200
the arrival of the first episode of REM sleep&nbsp;
in the cycling nature of sleep. And then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:58.200 --> 01:14:04.320
third aspect of that is it seems to decrease the&nbsp;
intensity of rapid eye movement sleep. And one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:04.320 --> 01:14:07.800
of the ways that we measure that is just how many&nbsp;
of these eye movements — that's where it gets its&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:07.800 --> 01:14:11.820
name by the way, rapid eye movement sleep because&nbsp;
your eyes dart back and forth — and the more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:11.820 --> 01:14:16.680
those rapid eye movements that you're having, the&nbsp;
more intense the REM sleep and, in fact, the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:16.680 --> 01:14:24.660
intense the dreaming and THC will decrease&nbsp;
the intensity, that metric of REM sleep too.&nbsp;

01:14:25.260 --> 01:14:30.000
I think perhaps the more powerful piece of&nbsp;
evidence for me looking at the literature that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:37.620
is REM sleep disruptive is less about the amount&nbsp;
or the quality of the REM sleep as you are using,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:37.620 --> 01:14:44.520
but what happens when you stop using, which is&nbsp;
all of a sudden you get a rebound effect where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:44.520 --> 01:14:51.060
you start to get significantly more REM sleep when&nbsp;
you stop using THC. And that's a demonstration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:51.060 --> 01:14:56.940
as a scientist, that we would say is a&nbsp;
homeostatic response, which is that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:56.940 --> 01:15:01.980
you deprive yourself of something, when you&nbsp;
get the chance to get it, you get an increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:01.980 --> 01:15:06.660
in the amount. There is a response. And that&nbsp;
increase, that homeostatic rebound effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:06.660 --> 01:15:12.227
is usually a sign of a deficiency that's been&nbsp;
happening before that. Does that make sense, Tim?&nbsp;

01:15:12.227 --> 01:15:17.280
Tim Ferriss: It does make sense. And if you could&nbsp;
allow me to indulge myself for a second here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.300 --> 01:15:25.080
I would say that in my personal experiences, and&nbsp;
this is an end of one anecdote and I understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:25.080 --> 01:15:32.100
that, but I've also used sleep trackers. So&nbsp;
I've used various devices. I have observed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:32.100 --> 01:15:38.700
over and over again, with more or less a hundred&nbsp;
percent predictability, the destructive effects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:38.700 --> 01:15:46.080
alcohol. It's so obvious. You feel miserable the&nbsp;
next day. You see it in the numbers. It's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:47.700 --> 01:15:51.840
incontrovertible evidence&nbsp;
that alcohol is not friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:51.840 --> 01:15:58.860
with my sleep, at least the quality of my sleep.
Now on the other hand, and I say this as someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:59.760 --> 01:16:05.580
who only began experimenting with cannabis&nbsp;
a few years ago — so I am not a lifelong&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:05.580 --> 01:16:11.700
pothead. This is not a religion to me. I have&nbsp;
no identity wrapped up in cannabis whatsoever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:11.700 --> 01:16:17.640
And for the most part, I had a very low opinion&nbsp;
of cannabis because I saw so many people become&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:19.320 --> 01:16:28.260
unproductive through overuse of cannabis over the&nbsp;
decades. But I have seen incredible effects on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:28.260 --> 01:16:34.200
sleep, and I've not observed, at least at the&nbsp;
dosing that I'm using — and as you mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.200 --> 01:16:38.940
Paracelsus' "The dose makes the poison." You need&nbsp;
to be careful with the dosing on all these things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:38.940 --> 01:16:46.440
including water, for instance. But it&nbsp;
hasn't seemed to inhibit my REM sleep.&nbsp;

01:16:47.220 --> 01:16:51.120
And I suppose there's also a question&nbsp;
that comes to mind, which is, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:51.120 --> 01:16:58.440
let's say it inhibited your REM sleep or&nbsp;
hypothetically, on average, it reduced REM sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:59.340 --> 01:17:07.680
10 percent. If it took you from 90 minutes&nbsp;
to fall asleep to 15 minutes to fall asleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:08.460 --> 01:17:14.580
could that end up in an absolute&nbsp;
increase in your REM sleep, even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:15.600 --> 01:17:19.860
theoretically it would decrease it percentage-wise&nbsp;
10 percent, if that makes any sense at all.&nbsp;

01:17:19.860 --> 01:17:22.200
Dr. Matt Walker: No, it does.
Tim Ferriss: So how would you suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:22.200 --> 01:17:32.100
we think about this? Because the dosing has always&nbsp;
been a question for me. How much does it take? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:32.100 --> 01:17:37.980
I could also just do an end of one experiment&nbsp;
where I do some on/off, as you mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:37.980 --> 01:17:44.460
to try to observe what happens. But do we have&nbsp;
any literature to point us in any direction with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:44.460 --> 01:17:48.180
respect to what type of dosing starts to suppress?
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah, it's a fantastic question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:48.180 --> 01:17:53.640
We don't right now in terms of looking at a&nbsp;
dose response curve. We probably have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:53.640 --> 01:17:58.800
dose response curve when it comes to the&nbsp;
benefit of reducing the time it takes you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:58.800 --> 01:18:05.100
to fall asleep. And there, going up starting at&nbsp;
2.5 milligrams all the way up to 20 milligrams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:05.100 --> 01:18:09.660
there is that sedating effect, and we start to&nbsp;
understand why there is that. Do we have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:09.660 --> 01:18:17.100
same data for REM sleep? We don't. Simply that&nbsp;
if you look at acute studies where you bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:17.100 --> 01:18:22.020
individuals into the lab, often they're cannabis&nbsp;
naive individuals, you dose them with THC —&nbsp;

01:18:22.020 --> 01:18:29.700
Tim Ferriss: You just bake the shit out of them.
Dr. Matt Walker: They are seeing visions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:29.700 --> 01:18:33.120
biblical characters in the sleep&nbsp;
laboratory before you put them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:33.120 --> 01:18:38.580
to sleep. No. So you can give them these —
I would say cut that part out. Leave it in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:38.580 --> 01:18:42.960
please do. Absolutely. Leave it in.
But what we have are these acute studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:42.960 --> 01:18:48.780
where when you dose acutely, you also certainly&nbsp;
get that reduction, that decrease in the amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:48.780 --> 01:18:55.680
of REM sleep. That's fairly reliable, fairly&nbsp;
consistent. But we don't have other studies where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:55.680 --> 01:19:02.100
we will then, say, do a longitudinal study where&nbsp;
we keep dosing people across a month and we track&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:02.100 --> 01:19:10.080
the evolution of these REM sleep impairments.
Could it be that there is also some homeostatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:10.080 --> 01:19:16.980
pushback where gradually the brain starts to&nbsp;
fight back against the deprivation of REM and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:16.980 --> 01:19:22.620
turn up the volume on REM sleep generation, and&nbsp;
therefore, you don't see the effects long term?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:22.620 --> 01:19:28.980
The only reason I would predict that that's not&nbsp;
the case is when you speak to lots of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:29.820 --> 01:19:36.240
who are chronic cannabis users and they&nbsp;
say that they stopped after one year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:36.240 --> 01:19:42.120
two years, they will say, "I just started&nbsp;
to have all of these crazy intense dreams&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:42.120 --> 01:19:44.280
as soon as I stopped smoking."
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, very common.&nbsp;

01:19:44.280 --> 01:19:48.660
Dr. Matt Walker: It's so common. And so I&nbsp;
think at that point, it still tells me that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:48.660 --> 01:19:57.840
the brain was bereft of REM sleep throughout that&nbsp;
period. It was undernourished. And by way of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:57.840 --> 01:20:04.320
nutrient deprivation of REM sleep, it had built&nbsp;
up this increasing hunger and desire for REM,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:04.320 --> 01:20:09.060
where as soon as you stopped the blocking&nbsp;
agent, which is the THC, it comes back with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:09.060 --> 01:20:14.640
a vengeance. That, I think, is the only evidence&nbsp;
that I know of right now that still tells me it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:14.640 --> 01:20:19.620
probably is having a suppressing REM sleep effect.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. And I will say that I have met&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:19.620 --> 01:20:25.980
a number of chronic users who have told me some&nbsp;
version of, I haven't remembered a dream in 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:25.980 --> 01:20:34.620
years, and then they stop and begin having these&nbsp;
extremely vivid dreams. And to your point, it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:34.620 --> 01:20:41.640
seem to have that effect. I have not personally&nbsp;
observed it. I think that may also be partially&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:41.640 --> 01:20:49.140
due to the fact that I try, or historically I&nbsp;
have tried, to cycle on and off very deliberately&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:49.140 --> 01:20:56.880
to avoid the development of tolerance, and I&nbsp;
have to imagine that that helps on some level.&nbsp;

01:20:56.880 --> 01:21:01.800
How does CBD fit into things, if at all?
Dr. Matt Walker: It is an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:01.800 --> 01:21:06.840
emerging story. So CBD, which is the&nbsp;
non-psychoactive component of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:07.560 --> 01:21:13.260
firstly emerged on the sleep map probably in the&nbsp;
1970s, much later than THC in the literature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:14.160 --> 01:21:19.980
It's actually really interesting. They were&nbsp;
dosing pigeons, Tim, would you believe it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:19.980 --> 01:21:25.020
with all of these different compounds. And they&nbsp;
were looking at the learning ability of pigeons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:25.020 --> 01:21:28.140
Turns out pigeons are actually very smart.&nbsp;
You can teach them things like the alphabet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:28.140 --> 01:21:32.700
And they were dosing them in the '70s with&nbsp;
different compounds. One of them was with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:34.080 --> 01:21:34.645
CBD, and they were using —
Tim Ferriss: Sorry, can I just pause?&nbsp;

01:21:34.645 --> 01:21:39.540
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: I'm imagining this experimenter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:39.540 --> 01:21:45.120
like the PI, going to the IRB and they're like,&nbsp;
"Can we do this in humans?" They're like, "No."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:45.120 --> 01:21:50.940
"Okay. Can we do this in monkeys?" "No." "Cats?"&nbsp;
"No." "Rats?" "No." "What the hell can we do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:50.940 --> 01:21:54.150
in?" "All right, we'll give you pigeons." And&nbsp;
they're like, "Ah, fine. We'll do it in pigeons."&nbsp;

01:21:54.150 --> 01:21:59.160
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah, I know. People seem&nbsp;
to think of pigeons as rats with wings. "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:59.160 --> 01:22:05.220
sure, you can work on them."
So they were looking at pigeons — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:05.220 --> 01:22:09.720
would love to be able to say that I'm a sleep&nbsp;
scientist and my specialty is in pigeon sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:09.720 --> 01:22:15.420
But they were studying pigeons. And what&nbsp;
they found was that with CBD exclusively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:16.920 --> 01:22:24.300
at higher doses I should say, the waking life&nbsp;
of those pigeons became a lot less wakeful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:24.300 --> 01:22:31.440
They started to demonstrate the expression&nbsp;
of slowed motoric movements. Their learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:31.440 --> 01:22:38.100
started to become worse. They demonstrated signs&nbsp;
of drowsiness. Now, by the way, they weren't high.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:38.100 --> 01:22:43.980
They weren't getting these pigeons baked because&nbsp;
there was no THC. It was just CBD components. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:43.980 --> 01:22:47.580
that was the first evidence that we had.
If you look at the human stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:48.480 --> 01:22:54.000
I don't think there's anywhere near enough data to&nbsp;
go on record to suggest that CBD is the Shangri-La&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:54.000 --> 01:23:01.620
of good sleep at night. It's mixed right now.&nbsp;
But I think the story is that in higher doses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:01.620 --> 01:23:10.200
CBD does seem to be able to potentially increase&nbsp;
the amount of deep non-REM sleep that one gets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:10.200 --> 01:23:15.420
if you look at some of the studies.
The concern, though, is that CBD seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:15.420 --> 01:23:19.860
to have a bimodal distribution. And what I&nbsp;
mean by that is in lower doses — and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:20.700 --> 01:23:24.660
I don't think there's good enough signs&nbsp;
to tell you exactly what those doses are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:24.660 --> 01:23:28.620
but perhaps if you squint your eyes&nbsp;
and you look at it slightly sideways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:28.620 --> 01:23:36.120
maybe it's about 25 milligrams or less of CBD.&nbsp;
And again, this is not me being medical in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:36.120 --> 01:23:41.460
of my advice or anything like that, as you said.&nbsp;
This is simply me being descriptive on the basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:41.460 --> 01:23:47.820
of the scientific data, not prescriptive in&nbsp;
terms of medical. But less than 25 milligrams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:47.820 --> 01:23:53.305
you actually get a wake-promoting effect&nbsp;
where CBD will actually make you more alert.&nbsp;

01:23:53.305 --> 01:23:57.660
Tim Ferriss: Oops.
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah, exactly. Whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.660 --> 01:24:04.140
in higher doses, maybe perhaps 50 milligrams or&nbsp;
above, it may have some sleep-inducing effects.&nbsp;

01:24:04.140 --> 01:24:09.420
Part of the reason that I think there's a lot of&nbsp;
variability out there is that, at least here in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:09.420 --> 01:24:15.084
the United States, it's not FDA-regulated and&nbsp;
there's a lot of variability from one batch.&nbsp;

01:24:15.084 --> 01:24:15.780
Tim Ferriss: Right.
Dr. Matt Walker: And if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:15.780 --> 01:24:21.360
really is so dose sensitive dependent that there's&nbsp;
this U-shape function, then you could be thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:21.360 --> 01:24:26.340
well, the bottle is telling me I take 50&nbsp;
milligrams every night and some nights I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:26.340 --> 01:24:31.980
struggle with sleep and other nights I get knocked&nbsp;
out. And I don't understand. It's not consistent.&nbsp;

01:24:33.420 --> 01:24:39.720
