02:02:04.723 --> 02:02:08.640
Dr. Matt Walker: So there are two&nbsp;
appetite-regulating hormones. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:08.640 --> 02:02:15.300
was called leptin, one was called ghrelin. Leptin,&nbsp;
by the way is, a hormone that says to your brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:15.300 --> 02:02:21.060
"Okay, don't eat it." We think of it almost like&nbsp;
the satiety signal that makes you — when you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:21.060 --> 02:02:26.100
had food, it makes you feel satisfied by your&nbsp;
food, and so you don't want to eat more. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:26.100 --> 02:02:31.980
ghrelin, on the other hand, it does the opposite.&nbsp;
It is the I-want-to-eat-more hormone. It increases&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:31.980 --> 02:02:34.560
your appetite. What researchers found —
Tim Ferriss: Ghrelin. G stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:34.560 --> 02:02:38.820
for "Go eat the cookies."
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah. Exactly. G for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:38.820 --> 02:02:44.940
"My stomach is growling and I desperately want&nbsp;
to eat more." What they found was that when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:44.940 --> 02:02:49.680
limit people to, let's say, just four to five&nbsp;
hours of sleep a night for several nights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:50.220 --> 02:02:56.340
you see a dastardly change in those two&nbsp;
appetite hormones. Firstly, what they found&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:56.340 --> 02:03:04.380
was that leptin, the I'm-satisfied-with-my-meal&nbsp;
satiety signal, that dropped by about 18 percent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:05.280 --> 02:03:10.620
If that wasn't bad enough, levels of ghrelin,&nbsp;
which is the hunger hormone, that increased by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:10.620 --> 02:03:16.740
28 percent. So in some ways, it's almost like&nbsp;
physiological double jeopardy. That you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:16.740 --> 02:03:22.200
getting punished twice for the same crime of not&nbsp;
sleeping. Once by a loss of the signal that says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:22.200 --> 02:03:27.060
"Don't eat. You're full with your food." You lose&nbsp;
that signal. And then, if that wasn't bad enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:27.060 --> 02:03:32.820
you ramp up your ghrelin signal, which is, "I'm&nbsp;
hungry." Hunger levels would increase by around&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:32.820 --> 02:03:41.460
about 24 to 25 percent, depending on the study.
So that was the emerging evidence on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:41.460 --> 02:03:48.300
appetite-regulating hormones. By the way, that&nbsp;
doesn't necessarily lead to a consistent change in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:48.300 --> 02:03:55.740
eating more of every type of food. The unfortunate&nbsp;
thing is that the class of food that you start&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:55.740 --> 02:04:01.080
to increase your greatest — it's not just that&nbsp;
you want to eat more. It's the things that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:01.080 --> 02:04:06.900
want to eat more of are the things that are more&nbsp;
obesogenic. So these are more carbohydrate-rich&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:06.900 --> 02:04:14.700
foods, simple-sugary foods, ice cream, sweets,&nbsp;
et cetera. Also, they found an increase in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:14.700 --> 02:04:20.220
preference for eating salty snacks, which can&nbsp;
put you towards a path of greater hypertension.&nbsp;

02:04:20.220 --> 02:04:26.220
So that was the evidence at the basic&nbsp;
appetite-regulating hormone level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:26.220 --> 02:04:32.880
But then the more recent data came out. You're&nbsp;
right. That all of us, even if we're not smoking&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:33.960 --> 02:04:39.780
the wacky tobacco, as you described, all of&nbsp;
us have our own cannabinoids that we release&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:39.780 --> 02:04:45.600
inside of our brain and our bodies. We have&nbsp;
naturally occurring cannabinoids in our brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:45.600 --> 02:04:49.500
and they're called the endocannabinoids.&nbsp;
Meaning that they come from inside of us, endo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:50.340 --> 02:04:56.760
And you're absolutely right that when you&nbsp;
give people exogenous cannabinoids like TCH,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:56.760 --> 02:05:02.340
it makes them hungry. You get the munchies. But&nbsp;
what they found was that when you sleep-deprived&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:02.340 --> 02:05:08.100
individuals, this has got nothing to do with&nbsp;
THC, or cannabis, or smoking weed, you increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:08.100 --> 02:05:18.240
the amount of these cannabinoids within the brain&nbsp;
and within individuals. So all of the sudden you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:18.240 --> 02:05:24.780
start to see, perhaps it's not just these leptin&nbsp;
and ghrelin that are changing to conspire to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:24.780 --> 02:05:30.360
increase your hunger and your waistline, but it's&nbsp;
also that these endocannabinoids are increasing.&nbsp;

02:05:31.140 --> 02:05:38.520
You can then start to ask, "Well, why? Why&nbsp;
would that be the case? How can we explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:38.520 --> 02:05:46.560
those things?" Currently, we don't quite know.&nbsp;
One hypothesis is that the only time when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:46.560 --> 02:05:52.080
see other species deprive themselves of sleep&nbsp;
— because human beings, in truth, are probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:52.080 --> 02:05:56.160
the only species that will deliberately deprive&nbsp;
themselves of sleep for no apparent good reason.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:56.160 --> 02:06:00.960
But animals will do it occasionally, and the&nbsp;
most common occurrence is when they're under&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:00.960 --> 02:06:08.880
conditions of starvation. So here, under those&nbsp;
conditions, the brain releases a wake-promoting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:08.880 --> 02:06:14.700
chemical called orexin. The reason is because&nbsp;
the brain has figured out, "I'm under conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:14.700 --> 02:06:20.700
of starvation. It must mean that my ability&nbsp;
to forage for food in my standard perimeter&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:20.700 --> 02:06:26.400
during the time that I'm awake is not yielding&nbsp;
enough food. So I need to push myself to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:26.400 --> 02:06:35.760
awake longer to forage in a wider perimeter, and&nbsp;
therefore to find and solve this caloric deficit."&nbsp;

02:06:35.760 --> 02:06:44.640
So one possibility is that because you are&nbsp;
deprived of sleep, you're getting this signal of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:44.640 --> 02:06:49.740
"Let me ramp up my hunger even more.&nbsp;
Let me drive up my motivation to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:49.740 --> 02:06:54.000
and search for food. Because otherwise,&nbsp;
I'm going to be an omission-critical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:54.600 --> 02:07:00.540
break-glass-in-case-of-emergency situation."&nbsp;
But we don't truly understand why these hormonal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:00.540 --> 02:07:08.400
patterns go awry. We certainly know that you will&nbsp;
increase your intake of food. We then went on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:08.400 --> 02:07:13.620
discover that it's not just about what changes&nbsp;
in your endocannabinoids or in your hormones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:13.620 --> 02:07:19.140
because in fact the choices that you make for your&nbsp;
foods are controlled by your brain. That's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:19.140 --> 02:07:25.800
central dominating decision pivot point for here.
So we took a group of individuals, and we deprived&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:25.800 --> 02:07:29.700
them of sleep, and then we gave them a full night&nbsp;
of sleep. And it was a counterbalance study, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:29.700 --> 02:07:35.520
they go through both full sleep and without sleep.&nbsp;
The next day we placed them inside an MRI scanner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:36.060 --> 02:07:43.080
and we started to show them different images&nbsp;
of food that range from really unhealthy foods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:45.000 --> 02:07:50.760
pizza, and donuts, and ice cream, all the&nbsp;
way up to really healthy different types&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:50.760 --> 02:07:56.880
of foods. And they had to rate the desire&nbsp;
that they had for those individual foods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:56.880 --> 02:08:02.820
We also did something a little bit dastardly to&nbsp;
make it more ecologically valid. We told them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:02.820 --> 02:08:07.500
and we did do this, that one of those items&nbsp;
that they said that they found desirable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:07.500 --> 02:08:13.500
we would then serve them when they came out of the&nbsp;
MRI scanner. And they had to, politely please, eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:13.500 --> 02:08:17.940
that food. So it sort of just forced them to make&nbsp;
sure that they're making the choices correctly.&nbsp;

02:08:17.940 --> 02:08:26.760
What we found is that the brain — you started&nbsp;
to rate these obesogenic foods as much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:26.760 --> 02:08:32.220
desirable. And the reason was that the&nbsp;
control regions of the brain, once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:32.220 --> 02:08:39.180
in the prefrontal cortex, were shut down. Whereas&nbsp;
more hedonic drive centers within the brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:39.180 --> 02:08:44.760
dopaminergic centers and also the amygdala,&nbsp;
which is also controlling hedonic food desires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:44.760 --> 02:08:52.260
those ramped up in terms of their activity.&nbsp;
That's a profile that we typically see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:52.920 --> 02:08:58.440
patients who are suffering from obesity. It's&nbsp;
this profile of what we would call a brain state&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:58.440 --> 02:09:04.140
of hedonic eating. That you shift over into&nbsp;
just going after desirous foods, unhealthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:04.140 --> 02:09:10.020
foods. So it was a two-part equation, changes&nbsp;
downstairs in the body and then changes upstairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:10.980 --> 02:09:17.280
within the brain. There have been since some&nbsp;
studies looking at dieting without sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:17.280 --> 02:09:21.300
which I think is probably some really fascinating&nbsp;
data, but let me just pause there for a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:21.300 --> 02:09:26.606
because I keep just talking. I get so excited.
Tim Ferriss: So, orexin. Is that O, R, E, X, I, N?&nbsp;

