[inaudible 02:12:48]. I'll take my customary five.
Dr. Matt Walker: [inaudible 02:12:49], is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:46.500 --> 02:09:49.860
assured after this conversation.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'll take my customary&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:49.860 --> 02:09:58.500
15 percent. That's fine. So orexin, I was asking&nbsp;
about that and also just contextualizing my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:58.500 --> 02:10:05.700
mind, everything you just said because I'd&nbsp;
love to get your read on just a amateurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:06.720 --> 02:10:14.640
play hypothesis, and this came about after,&nbsp;
unfortunately, my personal experience of many&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:14.640 --> 02:10:21.180
sleepless nights, lots of sleep deficit over&nbsp;
many decades, and have observed this eating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:23.160 --> 02:10:27.120
increased volume of intake, even if it's&nbsp;
healthier foods. If I'm trying to exert control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:27.120 --> 02:10:33.420
I'm still just eating a lot more and the drive&nbsp;
is so strong that I started to wonder what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:33.420 --> 02:10:39.240
evolutionary explanation for that might be.
Let's say this is an adaptive and not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:39.240 --> 02:10:47.160
pathological behavior, how would this be adaptive?&nbsp;
And I was thinking, and this may not hold any&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:47.160 --> 02:10:54.420
water, but I was thinking if you rob the body&nbsp;
of its ability to repair itself during sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:55.980 --> 02:11:05.220
could this not be a compensatory mechanism by&nbsp;
which you try to fix things by say, driving up&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:05.220 --> 02:11:09.960
anabolism? It's like, okay, you're not going to&nbsp;
give us the sleep that we need. Fine. We're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:09.960 --> 02:11:16.140
to try to turn on all the anabolic machinery&nbsp;
we can by throwing insulin into hyperdrive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:16.140 --> 02:11:21.240
by increasing caloric intake, which might have&nbsp;
some effect on testosterone or who knows what.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:23.100 --> 02:11:26.700
Do you think there might be&nbsp;
anything there or is that just me&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:27.240 --> 02:11:31.440
yelling nonsense into the wind as usual?
Dr. Matt Walker: I think it's certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:31.440 --> 02:11:36.300
one alternative hypothesis that&nbsp;
what you're trying to do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:36.960 --> 02:11:45.180
eat yourself in an overcoming way and to overcome&nbsp;
the sleep deprivation and see if you can placate&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:45.180 --> 02:11:50.100
some of what you are not getting by way of&nbsp;
sleep. I think that's one hypothesis. Right&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:50.100 --> 02:11:54.120
now I think the evidence is entertained in the&nbsp;
other direction, which is sort of flipping the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:54.120 --> 02:12:00.360
causal inference, which is that it's the lack of&nbsp;
sleep that your brain is receiving the signal of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:00.360 --> 02:12:05.700
which then suggests, oh, my goodness, I must be&nbsp;
under conditions of starvation. Because from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:05.700 --> 02:12:11.940
evolutionary standpoint, the only time that&nbsp;
Mother Nature ingrained in its biology has&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:11.940 --> 02:12:18.780
experienced insufficient sleep is under conditions&nbsp;
of starvation, and that's why you increase the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:18.780 --> 02:12:25.020
drive to eat. Other people have suggested it's&nbsp;
because of energy expenditure, but that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:25.020 --> 02:12:30.660
been largely dismissed. That's a myth, which is&nbsp;
that when you are awake, surely you are burning&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:30.660 --> 02:12:36.840
many more calories than when you are asleep.
And so the increase in food intake is simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:36.840 --> 02:12:44.700
trying to offset the increased metabolic&nbsp;
caloric expenditure. The problem is twofold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:45.600 --> 02:12:52.380
First, that sleep itself, metabolically is an&nbsp;
incredibly active process. We think of it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:52.380 --> 02:12:59.100
a passive process, but metabolically it's hugely&nbsp;
demanding. And in fact, the difference between you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:59.100 --> 02:13:07.620
lying on a couch doing nothing versus you sleeping&nbsp;
is a very small amount, meaning that sleep is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:07.620 --> 02:13:13.380
highly metabolic process. Second, if you look&nbsp;
at it across a full eight hour period of sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:13.380 --> 02:13:19.440
if you keep people awake, in particular chambers&nbsp;
where we can measure very concretely the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:19.440 --> 02:13:24.420
caloric and metabolic activity that's going&nbsp;
on and how many calories you're burning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:25.140 --> 02:13:32.280
what you find is that you will probably burn&nbsp;
only around about an extra 140 calories by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:32.280 --> 02:13:38.460
being sleep deprived all night than you would be&nbsp;
if you slept. However, the amount of increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:38.460 --> 02:13:44.340
calories that you take on board is usually at&nbsp;
least double or triple that, you will overeat by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:44.340 --> 02:13:51.600
somewhere in the region of three to 600 calories&nbsp;
