02:29:56.160 --> 02:30:02.340
the toolkit, especially for people who need it.
So let's talk about the name that has been invoked&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:02.340 --> 02:30:11.040
several times, and we may do it by speaking more&nbsp;
broadly about sleep drugs, the good, the bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:11.040 --> 02:30:16.800
and the ugly. But let's have it also include&nbsp;
trazodone since it's come up now at least twice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:17.399 --> 02:30:23.219
How would you suggest people think about&nbsp;
sleep medications and sleep architecture?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:25.140 --> 02:30:29.701
Where should we begin?
Dr. Matt Walker: It's really difficult. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:29.700 --> 02:30:36.059
just to state for the record, I'm not a medical&nbsp;
doctor and so this isn't anything sort of medical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:36.060 --> 02:30:39.661
and I've always been reticent to sort of&nbsp;
go on record, I suppose because of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:39.660 --> 02:30:45.120
because it's not clinical advice. But I can at&nbsp;
least try to offer some of the scientific evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:45.120 --> 02:30:51.120
regarding classic sleeping pills. And then maybe&nbsp;
more about the more novel compounds that you and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:51.120 --> 02:30:58.439
I have discussed. Right now, I'm probably not&nbsp;
a big advocate of the classic sleeping pills or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:58.439 --> 02:31:03.479
even the newer classic sleeping pills. And don't&nbsp;
necessarily need to name any names. Everyone can&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:03.479 --> 02:31:08.939
name them, but they're in a class of drugs that&nbsp;
we call the sedative hypnotics. And the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:08.939 --> 02:31:16.019
is that sedation is not sleep. And so the way that&nbsp;
those drugs work, those classic sleeping pills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:16.020 --> 02:31:20.760
is that they go after a chemical system&nbsp;
in the brain that's an inhibitory system.&nbsp;

02:31:20.760 --> 02:31:25.260
It's called the GABA system, which stands for&nbsp;
gamma aminobutyric acid, which is the major kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:25.260 --> 02:31:31.260
of red light stop signal in the brain. So when you&nbsp;
take those sleeping pills, I'm certainly not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:31.260 --> 02:31:37.500
to argue that you are awake at night, you're not.&nbsp;
But to argue that you are in naturalistic sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:37.500 --> 02:31:44.220
I think is perhaps an equal fallacy because if&nbsp;
I were to show you the electrical signature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:44.220 --> 02:31:48.900
your sleep with and without those classic&nbsp;
sleeping bills, they're not the same thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:49.680 --> 02:31:58.260
And one of the potential concerns is that if you&nbsp;
map out the electrical brainwave sort of signature&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:58.260 --> 02:32:03.540
of sleep, the spectrum of electrical brainwave&nbsp;
activity, you and I were discussing deep sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:03.540 --> 02:32:07.560
And it's particularly the deepest of the deep&nbsp;
slow brainwaves. And we can come onto some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:07.560 --> 02:32:13.500
the functions of those for learning and memory and&nbsp;
immune function, but where those classic sleeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:13.500 --> 02:32:20.280
pills will leave the most significant dent in&nbsp;
the quality of your sleep is particularly in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:20.280 --> 02:32:26.040
deepest of that deep sleep brainwave activity.
So it's taking a bite out of that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:27.600 --> 02:32:33.600
So that would perhaps be scientific cause for&nbsp;
being mindful about them. Now, by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:33.600 --> 02:32:38.580
current medical practice suggests that there&nbsp;
is a time and a place for those sleeping pills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:38.580 --> 02:32:41.819
but they are typically not&nbsp;
advocated long term. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:41.819 --> 02:32:46.380
please speak to your doctor, don't do&nbsp;
anything on the basis of my advice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:47.880 --> 02:32:54.480
I would say though, that one of the other aspects&nbsp;
of those sleeping medications is first that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:54.479 --> 02:33:02.160
typically develop tolerance. You also get some&nbsp;
pretty unfortunate side effects. Things like next&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:02.160 --> 02:33:08.160
day drowsiness, drowsy driving related accidents,&nbsp;
an increased risk of dementia. There's also been&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:08.160 --> 02:33:13.770
evidence that it increases your risk for falls&nbsp;
leading to hip fracture and skull fracture. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:13.770 --> 02:33:18.900
was an FDA warning about these sleep medications&nbsp;
recently that was published for those concerns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:19.740 --> 02:33:27.719
And due to the, I think back in 2016, the American&nbsp;
College of Physicians offered the recommendation&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:27.720 --> 02:33:32.460
that sleeping pills should not be the first line&nbsp;
treatment for insomnia, it should be something&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:32.460 --> 02:33:38.280
called cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia.
So I think they've fallen somewhat out of favor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:38.280 --> 02:33:43.859
If you were to come back to what I was telling&nbsp;
you, that they don't leave you with the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:43.859 --> 02:33:52.259
best quality of electrical sleep, they certainly&nbsp;
will increase classically the amount of sleep that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:52.260 --> 02:33:57.480
you have. And even, in fact, they can look like&nbsp;
they increase the amount of deep sleep that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:57.479 --> 02:34:01.919
get on the basis of classical sleep scoring.&nbsp;
But if we separate the brainwave activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:01.920 --> 02:34:07.319
then you start to see that the news is not&nbsp;
all good. But if you are sleeping longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:07.319 --> 02:34:12.899
shouldn't you get at least some benefit of&nbsp;
that? And the reason I became less bullish too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:12.899 --> 02:34:16.799
on those medications was because there's a great&nbsp;
study done by a colleague of mine, Marcos Frank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:16.800 --> 02:34:23.880
and he was dosing animals with different sleep&nbsp;
medications. And firstly, he was using a learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:23.880 --> 02:34:29.460
paradigm. And as the animals would learn, they&nbsp;
would create this memory trace in the brain that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:29.460 --> 02:34:34.920
he would map. And he could measure the strength&nbsp;
of the synaptic connections within the brain.&nbsp;

02:34:35.700 --> 02:34:40.740
Then, what would happen is that, if you let&nbsp;
those animals sleep naturally, the next day&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:40.740 --> 02:34:44.760
they would come back and the strength of&nbsp;
that memory trace, the strength of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:44.760 --> 02:34:49.800
synaptic connections had been increased even&nbsp;
more. And this is part of the reason why sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:49.800 --> 02:34:55.080
is so beneficial for learning and memory.
What he then did was dosed those animals&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:55.080 --> 02:35:00.840
with classic sleeping pills, one of which was&nbsp;
Zolpidem or Ambien. And firstly, those animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:00.840 --> 02:35:06.300
certainly they did sleep longer, which you&nbsp;
would think on the basis of then sleep's role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:06.300 --> 02:35:13.140
learning in memory and plasticity would mean that&nbsp;
the memory trace was even stronger the next day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:13.740 --> 02:35:20.760
However, what he found was that not only was the&nbsp;
memory trace, the memory circuit not strengthened&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:20.760 --> 02:35:27.960
by way of that sleeping pill induced sleep, it&nbsp;
was actually reduced. In fact, he found almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:27.960 --> 02:35:33.780
a 50 percent reduction, a weakening in the&nbsp;
strength of the memory trace as a consequence.&nbsp;

02:35:33.780 --> 02:35:39.599
So I think there's a, as I said, there's a time&nbsp;
and a place that clinical medicine believes there&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:39.600 --> 02:35:43.980
is a significant use case for these sleeping&nbsp;
medications. But I think in the long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:43.979 --> 02:35:50.219
the evidence is favoring against them and going&nbsp;
towards psychological treatments or other types&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:50.220 --> 02:35:56.220
of non-traditional sleep medications, things&nbsp;
like, as you mentioned, trazodone. We can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:56.220 --> 02:35:59.880
speak about pregabalin and gabapentin.
Tim Ferriss: Let's get after it. Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:59.880 --> 02:36:05.383
don't we keep the momentum? So which would you&nbsp;
like to put on the platter first? Trazodone?&nbsp;

02:36:05.382 --> 02:36:08.700
Dr. Matt Walker: I think probably&nbsp;
trazodone. Yeah. Right now, trazodone&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:08.700 --> 02:36:16.319
is probably the most off label prescribed sleep&nbsp;
medication. It's doesn't receive the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:17.580 --> 02:36:21.300
labeling for a sleep medication, but&nbsp;
it is used by many doctors off label.&nbsp;

02:36:21.300 --> 02:36:24.420
Trazodone, I find fascinating&nbsp;
for a number of reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:25.140 --> 02:36:30.720
It's originally designed as an anti-anxiety&nbsp;
or antidepressant medication in higher doses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:30.720 --> 02:36:36.781
But what we've discovered is that in lower&nbsp;
doses, and lower doses being anywhere between&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:38.280 --> 02:36:43.920
25 milligrams all the way up to maybe 300&nbsp;
milligrams, it's quite sleep inducing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:44.460 --> 02:36:51.780
And the reason I find it interesting is, firstly,&nbsp;
it does not work like classic sleeping pills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:51.780 --> 02:36:57.000
which, as I said, just knock out the cortex,&nbsp;
sedate the cortex by stimulating the inhibitory&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:57.000 --> 02:37:03.359
transmitter GABA. Instead, trazodone actually&nbsp;
works on the three neurochemical systems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:03.359 --> 02:37:07.920
we've mentioned before, which are the&nbsp;
wake-promoting systems within the brain.&nbsp;

02:37:07.920 --> 02:37:12.840
Specifically, trazodone will target the&nbsp;
noradrenaline system within the brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:12.840 --> 02:37:18.420
and it's what we call an alpha-1 adrenergic&nbsp;
antagonist, which simply means that it dials&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:18.420 --> 02:37:26.220
down the volume on noradrenaline. It is a 5-HT2A&nbsp;
antagonist, meaning it reduces serotonin activity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:26.220 --> 02:37:33.180
And finally, it reduces down histamine by&nbsp;
targeting the H1 receptor, which, again, we spoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:33.180 --> 02:37:38.520
about histamine being wake-promoting. If you block&nbsp;
it with anti-allergy medications, you get sleepy.&nbsp;

02:37:38.520 --> 02:37:45.480
By the way, trazodone, I know sounds quite scary.&nbsp;
It sounds like a tranquilizer. And it's not like&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:45.479 --> 02:37:51.660
that at all. It's actually very nuanced. And&nbsp;
what I like about its profile perhaps is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:51.660 --> 02:37:57.899
from a scientific perspective, is that it tries to&nbsp;
do something more naturalistic. It tries to switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:57.899 --> 02:38:05.339
off the volume of the wake-promoting regions and&nbsp;
therefore allows sleep, the passage of sleep to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:06.000 --> 02:38:09.899
be produced and arrive with&nbsp;
you in a more naturalistic way.&nbsp;

02:38:11.880 --> 02:38:16.560
Also, one of the other interesting features is&nbsp;
that not only does it — and by the way, it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:16.560 --> 02:38:21.180
to be quite effective. There was a meta-analysis&nbsp;
that was done recently on 11 different really&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:21.180 --> 02:38:29.281
well controlled, randomized placebo controlled&nbsp;
trials, or RCTs, across probably almost around 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:29.280 --> 02:38:36.479
different patients. And what they found probably&nbsp;
was three things of interest. The first is dose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:36.479 --> 02:38:44.160
then age, and then duration of use or efficacy.&nbsp;
They found that regardless of the dose, whether it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:44.160 --> 02:38:50.220
be below 50 milligrams or above 50 milligrams, it&nbsp;
provided benefits for reducing the time it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:50.220 --> 02:38:55.500
to fall asleep, which is what you were describing&nbsp;
with trazodone use. It reduces the amount of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:55.500 --> 02:39:02.460
you then are spending awake for the rest of&nbsp;
the night. It increased deep non-REM sleep.&nbsp;

02:39:02.460 --> 02:39:07.319
But here was the fascinating part for me,&nbsp;
it didn't come at the cost of REM sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:08.100 --> 02:39:13.680
which in some ways is a little bit surprising.&nbsp;
They're not utterly antagonistic in their role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:13.680 --> 02:39:18.720
REM and non-REM. But I mentioned earlier on&nbsp;
in the conversation that you can increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:18.720 --> 02:39:23.460
deep non-REM sleep by way of exercise.&nbsp;
But if you look at some of those sleeves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:23.460 --> 02:39:29.939
when you increase deep non-REM, you can have&nbsp;
an exercise induced reduction in REM sleep.&nbsp;

02:39:30.720 --> 02:39:35.100
But yet what's interesting about trazodone&nbsp;
is that it seems to increase the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:35.100 --> 02:39:41.880
deep non-REM sleep, but leaves REM sleep&nbsp;
untouched. You don't get a consequence to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:41.880 --> 02:39:46.080
REM sleep. And we don't quite understand why&nbsp;
that is, but that's one of the nice things&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:46.080 --> 02:39:51.240
is that whether it be below 50 milligrams or&nbsp;
above 50 milligrams, you get these benefits.&nbsp;

02:39:51.240 --> 02:39:59.519
The age dependency is quite nice, because if you&nbsp;
look at people who are younger than 60 years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:59.520 --> 02:40:05.640
you get these sleep benefits. But even you see&nbsp;
many, not all of them, but many of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:05.640 --> 02:40:11.520
sleep benefits for people who are 60 years&nbsp;
or older. And we know that as we get older,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:11.520 --> 02:40:17.701
as you and I discussed earlier, the harder that&nbsp;
sleep becomes. And so that could arguably be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:17.700 --> 02:40:24.179
more challenging use case where that medication&nbsp;
would fail, yet you seem to get quite a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:24.180 --> 02:40:29.701
the distance and the benefit even as you get&nbsp;
older. So I think that's a potential upside.&nbsp;

02:40:30.899 --> 02:40:36.479
And then the other aspect of it was the duration&nbsp;
of action. They did look at this, which comes onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:36.479 --> 02:40:43.500
aspects of tolerance and withdrawal. There didn't&nbsp;
necessarily seem to be a significant tolerance&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:43.500 --> 02:40:50.580
buildup, where if you look at acute dosing for&nbsp;
one week, you get these nice benefits. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:50.580 --> 02:40:55.500
if you keep assessing people week after week&nbsp;
up to one month, at least in the meta-analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:55.500 --> 02:41:00.960
the benefits were still there for the most part,&nbsp;
which suggests that there isn't necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:00.960 --> 02:41:06.000
tolerance to the drug, that those benefits&nbsp;
persist even when you continue to take the drug.&nbsp;

02:41:06.000 --> 02:41:11.880
So right now, that's why I think it's an&nbsp;
interesting drug in terms of its profile&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:11.880 --> 02:41:17.100
and how it works. So I'm not anti-medication.&nbsp;
Please don't think that. It's just that if those&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:17.100 --> 02:41:21.180
medications aren't producing naturalistic&nbsp;
sleep, I prefer to say so. But here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:21.180 --> 02:41:26.220
it seems to be a very different mechanism.
Tim Ferriss: So now, to those listening, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:26.220 --> 02:41:32.580
might say, "Well, wait a minute. Dr. Walker, you&nbsp;
have said there is no biological free lunch. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

