02:46:37.260 --> 02:46:40.860
Tim Ferriss: Got it. And I should&nbsp;
issue a warning here, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:42.060 --> 02:46:47.100
work with professionals before you start combining&nbsp;
drugs or taking any drugs for that matter, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:48.359 --> 02:46:52.259
if you don't read the fine print on what I'm&nbsp;
saying, you can get yourself into trouble.&nbsp;

02:46:52.260 --> 02:47:00.900
So to give an example, there are some&nbsp;
cases where SSRIs will decrease the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:01.920 --> 02:47:07.319
hallucinogenic effects, psychedelic effects&nbsp;
of given compounds. There are other cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:09.060 --> 02:47:15.000
maybe not most notably, but notably in the case&nbsp;
of ayahuasca, where if you combine psychiatric&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:15.000 --> 02:47:20.880
medications with ayahuasca, you could actually&nbsp;
suffer from a potentially highly dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:20.880 --> 02:47:28.740
serotonin syndrome. And so instead of reducing the&nbsp;
effect, you would be compounding the effects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:29.280 --> 02:47:33.599
the experience. So you've got to be very, very,&nbsp;
very careful with this stuff. You can get yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:33.600 --> 02:47:37.003
into deep water very quickly and get yourself&nbsp;
into trouble. So work with [inaudible 02:58:48].&nbsp;

02:47:37.003 --> 02:47:40.320
Dr. Matt Walker: I think that's actually a&nbsp;
really important point, which is the interactive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:40.319 --> 02:47:42.519
what we call polypharmacy.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:47:42.519 --> 02:47:46.019
Dr. Matt Walker: And a more benign version of&nbsp;
that, or perhaps a less malignant is a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:46.020 --> 02:47:51.480
way of describing it, version of that actually&nbsp;
comes back to our discussion of caffeine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:51.479 --> 02:47:58.919
There is some emerging evidence that using&nbsp;
SSRIs increases your sensitivity to caffeine&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:58.920 --> 02:48:05.880
and it decreases the clearance of caffeine.&nbsp;
And so you can think, "Well, I'm this type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:05.880 --> 02:48:07.423
person with my caffeine use."
Tim Ferriss: Interesting.&nbsp;

02:48:07.422 --> 02:48:10.620
Dr. Matt Walker: And then you&nbsp;
can start taking these SSRIs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:11.580 --> 02:48:16.200
which then all of a sudden you realize, "I'm not&nbsp;
sleeping. I'm not sleeping as well as I used to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:16.200 --> 02:48:21.840
And I don't think it's because of the caffeine&nbsp;
intake, because my caffeine intake has remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:21.840 --> 02:48:26.640
stable. I haven't changed that." But what's&nbsp;
changed is the interaction between those SSRIs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:26.640 --> 02:48:33.180
ramping up the duration and the severity of action&nbsp;
of caffeine. So you have to be really thoughtful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:33.180 --> 02:48:34.543
and that's where it gets very complex.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:48:34.543 --> 02:48:37.247
Dr. Matt Walker: That's where us human&nbsp;
beings are desperately messy things.&nbsp;

02:48:37.247 --> 02:48:43.800
Tim Ferriss: So messy. Wow. What a tangled web&nbsp;
we weave. What a sad situation it would be if&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:43.800 --> 02:48:52.920
someone were using trazodone for sleep and&nbsp;
yet experienced this dramatic magnification&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:52.920 --> 02:48:58.237
of their experience with caffeine and netted&nbsp;
out in the red. That would be a real bummer.&nbsp;

02:48:58.237 --> 02:49:01.219
Dr. Matt Walker: Well, yeah. We don't&nbsp;
yet know if it's trazodone in particular.&nbsp;

02:49:01.219 --> 02:49:01.800
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Yeah.
Dr. Matt Walker: These were&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:01.800 --> 02:49:03.435
the more classic SSRIs.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Trazodone&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:03.434 --> 02:49:04.919
is pretty weak. Pretty weak.
Dr. Matt Walker: So right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:04.920 --> 02:49:06.951
jury is out.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, pretty —&nbsp;

02:49:06.951 --> 02:49:07.925
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Well, yeah. It's sort of a —&nbsp;

02:49:07.924 --> 02:49:08.700
Dr. Matt Walker: Exactly.
Tim Ferriss: — a failed — I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:08.700 --> 02:49:12.019
want to call it a failed antidepressant,&nbsp;
but it's like by the time it starts to —&nbsp;

02:49:12.020 --> 02:49:13.885
Dr. Matt Walker: No. It's certainly&nbsp;
not a first line, yeah, treatment.&nbsp;

02:49:13.885 --> 02:49:14.760
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Dr. Matt Walker: And that's why it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:14.760 --> 02:49:17.840
become — its most popular use right now —
Tim Ferriss: People just fall asleep.&nbsp;

02:49:17.840 --> 02:49:18.742
Dr. Matt Walker: — would be&nbsp;
not as an antidepressant.&nbsp;

02:49:18.742 --> 02:49:19.259
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.
Dr. Matt Walker: It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:19.260 --> 02:49:25.260
because it's, yeah, sleep-inducing. Yep.
Tim Ferriss: All right. So let's leapfrog&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:25.260 --> 02:49:30.480
to pregabalin and gabapentin. You can take either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:31.500 --> 02:49:37.380
Gabapentin, people may recognize because&nbsp;
veterinarians love to hand it out like Pez candy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:37.380 --> 02:49:42.403
when you take your dog in or your cat in.
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:49:42.403 --> 02:49:44.819
Tim Ferriss: But which of those&nbsp;
would you like to talk about first?&nbsp;

02:49:45.540 --> 02:49:47.939
Dr. Matt Walker: We can talk about&nbsp;
both of them, because in some ways —&nbsp;

02:49:47.939 --> 02:49:49.439
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Great.
Dr. Matt Walker: — they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:49.439 --> 02:49:52.531
part of the same class of —
Tim Ferriss: Perfect.&nbsp;

02:49:52.532 --> 02:49:56.340
Dr. Matt Walker: — drugs, pregabalin and&nbsp;
gabapentin. You're right that they're used&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:56.340 --> 02:50:04.260
for multiple different reasons, one of which is&nbsp;
pain medication, muscle relaxant. Neurologically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:04.260 --> 02:50:10.740
they're often used in seizure disorders, because&nbsp;
they will reduce down the seizure tendency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:11.399 --> 02:50:16.979
And you would think by the names pregabalin&nbsp;
and gabapentin that they're going to act by way&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:16.979 --> 02:50:21.899
of targeting, once again, the GABA system, that&nbsp;
inhibitory system. And so they're sedatives, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:21.899 --> 02:50:27.899
so you don't seem to be a big fan of those, Matt.
But it turns out that that's not the case. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:27.899 --> 02:50:29.404
shouldn't be fooled. They're actually calcium&nbsp;
channel — they alter the functioning of calcium&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:29.404 --> 02:50:31.319
channels, which in part — I'll start that&nbsp;
again. They actually impact the functioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:31.319 --> 02:50:37.019
of calcium channels on neurons, and those&nbsp;
calcium channels will dictate the firing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:37.020 --> 02:50:42.720
of those neurons. And so you can reduce down&nbsp;
the firing propensity of those neurons. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:42.720 --> 02:50:48.660
why they're given in seizure disorders, in&nbsp;
epilepsy, to try to suppress the excessive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:48.660 --> 02:50:54.540
this hyperpolarization, this hyperactivity of the&nbsp;
brain that creates the epicenter of the seizure.&nbsp;

02:50:54.540 --> 02:51:00.479
If you look at the evidence, it's interesting.&nbsp;
When you dose both naive individuals, meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:00.479 --> 02:51:06.059
naive patients who've never received either of&nbsp;
these drugs or don't have seizure disorders or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:06.060 --> 02:51:13.260
sleep disorders, they both do seem to increase&nbsp;
the amount of deep non-REM sleep and they do&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:13.260 --> 02:51:21.840
seem to reduce the amount of light stage one,&nbsp;
non-REM sleep. So I think they are interesting.&nbsp;

02:51:21.840 --> 02:51:27.660
I think there's probably indirect mechanisms, too.&nbsp;
They certainly have a relaxing property. They can&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:27.660 --> 02:51:35.220
be a muscle relaxant. They can reduce some degree&nbsp;
of anxiety. We also know that those drugs will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:35.220 --> 02:51:41.040
decrease down both the fight or flight branch of&nbsp;
the nervous system and what's called the HPA axis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:41.040 --> 02:51:47.880
the stress related cortisol axis. So I think part&nbsp;
of it is because they reduce down anxiety, which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:47.880 --> 02:51:52.500
as we've described before, is not great&nbsp;
for sleep. So it's an indirect mechanism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:52.500 --> 02:51:57.960
And then the other component is that it may&nbsp;
actually have some, unknown to us right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:57.960 --> 02:52:04.140
neuronal impacts by way of calcium channel&nbsp;
alteration that increases deep sleep.&nbsp;

02:52:04.140 --> 02:52:08.460
But right now, I don't know of anyone who's pulled&nbsp;
apart that mechanism within the brain itself.&nbsp;

02:52:08.460 --> 02:52:13.800
Tim Ferriss: Question on pregabalin. So&nbsp;
when I first used pregabalin, so I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:13.800 --> 02:52:21.960
been using trazodone as a monotherapy and then&nbsp;
introduced pregabalin, I experienced — and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:22.680 --> 02:52:28.741
very noticeable, could just be placebo effect, but&nbsp;
I wasn't anticipating it, which is why, unless I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:28.740 --> 02:52:36.540
just seeing things, it wasn't expectancy effect,&nbsp;
because I wasn't looking for it. But I seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:36.540 --> 02:52:46.500
notice a carryover effect of lowered anxiety, so&nbsp;
an anxiolytic effect all the way through maybe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:46.500 --> 02:52:55.560
first five, six hours of my waking subsequent day&nbsp;
after using pregabalin pre-bed. Is that a mirage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:55.560 --> 02:53:01.320
or is there a plausible explanation for that?
Dr. Matt Walker: No. I think there is a plausible&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:01.319 --> 02:53:06.959
explanation. It actually has quite a — well,&nbsp;
not a quite short, but a somewhat short half&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:06.959 --> 02:53:13.200
life of about five to six hours. So when&nbsp;
you come back then to the quarter life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:13.200 --> 02:53:19.380
we're talking about somewhere around 12 hours. So&nbsp;
it would make sense that it's in the first part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:19.380 --> 02:53:27.240
the day where you still get that floaty, reduced&nbsp;
jittery feeling in the morning, where things just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:27.240 --> 02:53:33.479
feel a little bit more relaxed, because that still&nbsp;
is present in your system. It depends on the dose.&nbsp;

02:53:34.140 --> 02:53:39.241
By the way, pregabalin and gabapentin&nbsp;
in the sleep field are principally used&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:39.240 --> 02:53:44.580
not for insomnia, but actually to treat a&nbsp;
sleep disorder called restless leg syndrome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:44.580 --> 02:53:50.220
which is a horrific disorder where you get these,&nbsp;
in America, I think you call them Charliehorses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:50.220 --> 02:53:55.980
but in England, we'll just get these creepy crawly&nbsp;
feelings in your muscles and you have to move, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:55.979 --> 02:54:01.439
have to stretch them. It disrupts sleep terribly.&nbsp;
It's a pretty tough sleep disorder. And it's used&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:01.439 --> 02:54:07.200
there to try to treat some of the aspects of&nbsp;
the muscle cramps and the muscle problems.&nbsp;

02:54:08.160 --> 02:54:14.580
There, you can go up to doses, I think&nbsp;
people can use up to 225 milligrams of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:15.540 --> 02:54:20.580
pregabalin, gabapentin,&nbsp;
maybe up to 600 milligrams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:21.420 --> 02:54:26.100
So there, you can get lingering after effects&nbsp;
that are not only a reduced level of anxiety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:26.100 --> 02:54:32.760
but also an increase in sleepiness that&nbsp;
people don't like it that they have next&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:32.760 --> 02:54:37.500
morning grogginess and hangover effect. So one&nbsp;
has to be a little bit thoughtful for dose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:37.500 --> 02:54:42.000
I think. But it makes complete sense based on the&nbsp;
half life of what you're describing there. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:42.000 --> 02:54:45.960
would still get some of that benefit.
Tim Ferriss: So I do know someone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:45.960 --> 02:54:53.380
I don't want to name names here, I'll&nbsp;
protect the guilty, but who is a —&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:55.319 --> 02:55:01.380
let's call this person a pharmacological champion.&nbsp;
They have high tolerance for everything. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:02.040 --> 02:55:05.460
pregabalin forced them to tap out.
Dr. Matt Walker: Wow. Wow.&nbsp;

02:55:05.460 --> 02:55:11.520
Tim Ferriss: In the sense that they&nbsp;
were so laid out — not laid out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:11.520 --> 02:55:16.064
but altered the next day. They were like,&nbsp;
"Okay. That was too much. That was too much."&nbsp;

02:55:16.063 --> 02:55:19.800
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: I do not have that experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:19.800 --> 02:55:29.400
personally, but I do find it to be a fascinating&nbsp;
drug. It really is a fascinating drug. And I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:29.399 --> 02:55:35.339
want to become too dependent on it. I should take&nbsp;
a step back and also say, lest people think that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:35.340 --> 02:55:43.140
am just swallowing pills all day to wake up and&nbsp;
then feel X, not feel Y, and then go to sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:43.800 --> 02:55:52.560
what I find is that if I simply have these drugs&nbsp;
available and I put them on my nightstand and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:52.560 --> 02:55:57.060
"If I'm not asleep in 20 minutes or 30 minutes,&nbsp;
I'm going to give myself permission to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:57.779 --> 02:56:02.700
some of these sleep aids," that's enough a&nbsp;
lot of the time. Just knowing it's there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:03.479 --> 02:56:07.559
having a rule in place often allows&nbsp;
me to get to sleep without anything.&nbsp;

02:56:07.560 --> 02:56:12.060
Dr. Matt Walker: I think this is such a powerful&nbsp;
message, thanks for bringing it up, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:14.040 --> 02:56:18.840
it's almost this strange placebo effect of&nbsp;
knowing security is there, because when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:18.840 --> 02:56:25.680
suffer from insomnia, and I've been there too,&nbsp;
one of the miserable parts is that you lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:25.680 --> 02:56:28.003
all faith in your ability to sleep.
Tim Ferriss: One hundred percent.&nbsp;

02:56:28.003 --> 02:56:33.600
Dr. Matt Walker: In other words, your sleep&nbsp;
controls you. You do not control your sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:34.200 --> 02:56:39.599
And by way of these types of medications,&nbsp;
that's why I'm not completely anti-medication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:39.600 --> 02:56:45.840
I think there's enormous value in you regaining&nbsp;
your confidence that every time I go into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:45.840 --> 02:56:50.100
thing called my bedroom, because when you're in&nbsp;
insomnia — if you never suffer from insomnia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:50.100 --> 02:56:55.800
you would never think this. But when you walk into&nbsp;
the bedroom, it's already a trigger. You already&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:55.800 --> 02:57:00.120
start to get negative emotions. You look at the&nbsp;
bed and you think, "I know what's going to happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:00.120 --> 02:57:08.160
I am going to have a miserable night of sleep."
And just to have a pharmacologically-assured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:09.540 --> 02:57:14.760
it's not guaranteed, but certainly higher&nbsp;
probability of being able to get into bed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:14.760 --> 02:57:19.320
fall asleep, at that point you've turned&nbsp;
the tables. Now you control your sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:19.319 --> 02:57:27.540
rather than your sleep controls you. That freedom,&nbsp;
that release from the incarceration of the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:28.200 --> 02:57:33.660
I think, vicious condition of insomnia is&nbsp;
incredibly powerful. And I'm so glad that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:33.660 --> 02:57:35.683
that's what you experience.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:57:35.683 --> 02:57:40.439
Dr. Matt Walker: It's a miserable place to be.
Tim Ferriss: It's a huge boon just to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:40.439 --> 02:57:48.540
that you have something reliable that can brute&nbsp;
force the problem towards a solution if needed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:49.740 --> 02:57:55.920
Is not to suggest that I always want to brute&nbsp;
force the solution. And you and I have also&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:55.920 --> 02:58:02.100
spoken about this, where there are tools out there&nbsp;
like CBTI, right, cognitive behavioral therapy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:02.700 --> 02:58:13.080
for insomnia, that are extremely valuable.
But if your mind has suddenly become on some level&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:14.340 --> 02:58:23.880
or is, I don't want to say the enemy, but&nbsp;
if the mental self-talk is such that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:23.880 --> 02:58:29.220
walk into the bedroom, you try to sleep, you're&nbsp;
failing to sleep, you start berating yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:29.220 --> 02:58:34.380
you get angry, then you start to worry about&nbsp;
what the next day is going to look like, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:34.380 --> 02:58:39.660
so now your heart rate is 30 percent faster, your&nbsp;
cortisol is being released, and on and on it goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:39.660 --> 02:58:45.479
in this vicious cycle that further ensures you&nbsp;
are not going to sleep, knowing that you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:46.500 --> 02:58:53.040
the emergency option, which will&nbsp;
put you to sleep, is a tremendous&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:53.700 --> 02:59:01.380
stress reliever. And I have found that just&nbsp;
having it there, just having the break glass in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:01.380 --> 02:59:07.560
case of emergency pills on the nightstand allow me&nbsp;
oftentimes to sleep without taking anything. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:07.560 --> 02:59:09.623
it's just the security of knowing it's there.
Dr. Matt Walker: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:59:09.622 --> 02:59:10.260
Tim Ferriss: And —
Dr. Matt Walker: I'm so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:10.260 --> 02:59:14.700
glad that that's, yeah, that's the case for you.&nbsp;
And I am also a big advocate of CBTI, cognitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:14.700 --> 02:59:20.340
behavioral therapy for insomnia, which is just&nbsp;
as efficacious as classic things like Ambien.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:20.340 --> 02:59:27.779
But I would also note that it's not uncommon that&nbsp;
people combine both cognitive behavioral therapy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:27.779 --> 02:59:33.660
which is the psychological treatment that people&nbsp;
can look up, CBTI, together with these types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:33.660 --> 02:59:38.939
sleep medications to produce the very best&nbsp;
benefits. And then at some point, maybe you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:38.939 --> 02:59:46.200
start to taper down the pharmacology, maintain the&nbsp;
cognitive behavioral therapy components, and lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:46.200 --> 02:59:53.099
people out into that pathway of confidence&nbsp;
with their sleep. But it's just having that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:53.100 --> 02:59:58.621
reassurance can make the world of difference.
And the other aspect with pharmacology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:58.620 --> 03:00:05.340
and I know it maybe goes against the idea of no&nbsp;
free lunches, sometimes in medicine and science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:05.340 --> 03:00:11.160
we think of taking medicines as putting us&nbsp;
out of balance. And that's the mentality that&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:11.160 --> 03:00:16.800
people have, I think rightly so, as to their&nbsp;
resistance against pharmacology. Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:16.800 --> 03:00:21.360
though, a way that I think about it is that&nbsp;
whatever condition that you are in that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:21.359 --> 03:00:27.299
led you to think about pharmacology probably means&nbsp;
that you are biologically and physiologically out&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:27.300 --> 03:00:32.640
of balance. And what those medications are doing&nbsp;
is actually putting you back into a more natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:32.640 --> 03:00:38.701
biological balance rather than the inverse&nbsp;
view that perhaps some people rightly have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:38.700 --> 03:00:43.859
which is, "When I take these drugs, I'm putting&nbsp;
myself into an artificial, unnatural imbalance."&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:43.859 --> 03:00:47.880
That may not necessarily be the case.
Tim Ferriss: That's a great observation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:47.880 --> 03:00:54.840
And I think this is also a pretty good place&nbsp;
to start landing the plane. And I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:54.840 --> 03:00:59.520
if you'd be open to this, so you can listen to&nbsp;
my ramblings for another two and a half hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:59.520 --> 03:01:03.703
and put up with my questions, but would you&nbsp;
be open to doing a round two at some point?&nbsp;

03:01:03.703 --> 03:01:06.874
Dr. Matt Walker: Oh, just sign me up —
Tim Ferriss: All right. All right. Beautiful.&nbsp;

03:01:06.873 --> 03:01:07.532
Dr. Matt Walker: — next time. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So let me —&nbsp;

03:01:07.533 --> 03:01:10.560
Dr. Matt Walker: Any time, I would love to.
Tim Ferriss: So let me give people — if&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:10.560 --> 03:01:14.161
it's okay with you, I'm going to give&nbsp;
people a teaser of what we might cover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:15.359 --> 03:01:21.120
This is a non-exhaustive list, but&nbsp;
there are a number of things that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:21.120 --> 03:01:26.700
brainstormed beforehand. I do not know the answers&nbsp;
to these things and I would really love to explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:26.700 --> 03:01:31.800
them. So we have sleep, learning, memory, and&nbsp;
creativity, and there are many bullets to explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:31.800 --> 03:01:37.740
there. We have sleep and sex, intercourse, orgasm,&nbsp;
masturbation, libido, all my favorite things. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:37.740 --> 03:01:43.319
we can talk about that next time around in round&nbsp;
two. And then also dreaming and lucid dreaming. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:43.319 --> 03:01:48.120
I have a long standing, multi-decade long&nbsp;
interest in lucid dreaming and I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:48.120 --> 03:01:53.519
want to dig into dreaming and lucid dreaming.
So those are just a few of the things that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:53.520 --> 03:02:02.041
and should explore in round two. Matt, is there&nbsp;
anything you would like to mention in closing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

